      <work osisWork="bible">
        <title>Referenced versification (standard)</title>
        <identifier type="OSIS">bible</identifier>
        <language>en</language>
        <refSystem>StandardV11N</refSystem>
      </work>
    </header>
    <div type="book" osisID="Rev" canonical="true">
        <chapter sID="Rev.1" osisID="Rev.1" n="1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.1" osisID="Rev.1.1" n="1"/>These are the mysteries given by Yehovah <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="20" osisRef="Rev.1.1" osisID="Rev.1.1!note.20">The message originates directly from Yehovah to Yeshua through John to the servants of Yehovah. And in all of Cochin Hebrew Revelation, Yehovah’s name is included in these scriptures. For by knowing and calling upon Yehovah’s Name, there is power. In Joel 2:32 says that by calling upon the Name of Yehovah, there is deliverance. Psalms 91:14-16 also demonstrates that by the one who knows Yehovah’s Name brings deliverance, esteem, and success. That is why there is a great effort to remove and conceal the Name of Yehovah. For Yehovah’s Name was removed from English translations 6,823 times (The Scriptures 2009, pp xv). In Exodus 20:7, we are told not to bring Yehovah’s Name to לַשָּׁוְא (la’sha’u) meaning, “to nothing, emptiness, nothingness, vanity, falsehood.” In other words, we are to know and speak the Name of Yehovah and not bring His Name to nothingness. See Revelation 7:4 and the 144,000 teaching the Name of Yehovah.</note>to Yeshua the Messiah, to show his servants what would soon happen. And He sent them by the hand of his messenger to his servant, John.<verse eID="Rev.1.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.2" osisID="Rev.1.2" n="2"/>The witness is the Word of Yehovah and the testimony <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="24" osisRef="Rev.1.2" osisID="Rev.1.2!note.24">“Testimony” עדות (edut) is also referring to the Torah as in the “Ark of the Testimony” described in Exodus 16:34, 35, 36. This is translated עדות as testimony, not congregations (plural) for it fits the context of the witness of the Torah.</note>of Yeshua the Messiah and of what he saw.<verse eID="Rev.1.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.3" osisID="Rev.1.3" n="3"/>Holy <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="26" osisRef="Rev.1.3" osisID="Rev.1.3!note.26">The Hebrew word kadosh ( קדוש ) means, “set-apart” and is commonly translated as “holy, sanctified, consecrated.”</note>are the reader <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="27" osisRef="Rev.1.3" osisID="Rev.1.3!note.27">This word can also be “one who announces” or “one who proclaims.” Notice there is one announcer (singular) and there are multiple hearers.</note>and the hearers of these prophecies and those establishing what is written.<verse eID="Rev.1.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.4" osisID="Rev.1.4" n="4"/>John and the seven elders in Asia minor, grace, and peace to you from He who is, and was, and will be, and from the seven Spirits<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="30" osisRef="Rev.1.4" osisID="Rev.1.4!note.30">See Isaiah 11:1-5 for the names of the seven Spirits of Yehovah. Also consider that in Isaiah 11:4-5 it describes in detail the seventh Spirit of Yehovah as One who is a “right ruler.” Then, in Isaiah 28:5-6, the “Spirit of Right-Ruling” is directly named and will manifest in the end of days. Thus, the Spirit of Right-Ruling appears to be the Seventh Spirit of Yehovah for Yehovah is One who encompasses all seven Spirits. Also notice that in Revelation 5:6, the seven Spirits of Yehovah are sent ( שלח - shalach) into all the earth and represents Yeshua the Messiah. How will the seven Spirits be identified? By the gifts they present. The seven Spirits of Yehovah will be rare events and manifestations of Yeshua the Messiah. Also consider that the seven Spirits of Yehovah appears to be different than the Holy Spirit ( רוח הקודש – Ruach Ha’Kodesh) for the Holy Spirit is poured out ( שפך – shafach) and has different spiritual gifts (Joel 2:28-29, Acts 2:1-4). Therefore, In Revelation 5:6 when the seven Spirits of Yehovah are sent into all the earth, this represents the manifestation of Yeshua. And interestingly, in the Cochin Hebrew 1 Corinthians 15:23, it mentions the day when Yeshua returns describing his return as: אדם אדם בדתו ראשית הוא במשיח ובסוף אלו של משיח הם גם כך בבואו .֞ This verse translates as, “Adam, a man's (adam) first law/covenant is in Messiah. And in the end, those belonging to Messiah are also the same at his coming,” (emphasis mine). The Hebrew word בבואו (be’bo’o) means, “at/in/with/by his coming,” and is prefixed with the bet (ב) and not a kaf (כ) prefix. The difference is that with the bet (ב) prefix it makes Yeshua’s coming a continual and progressive action. The kaf (כ) prefix makes it an action just performed. Therefore, Yeshua is “continually coming,” through the seven Spirts of Yehovah and in the end, we will see Him as described in Zechariah 12:10, Revelation 1:7 and in Matthew 24:30b.</note> that are from His throne.<verse eID="Rev.1.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.5" osisID="Rev.1.5" n="5"/>And from Yeshua the Messiah, the faithful witness and the first One resurrected from the dead and the Messiah and Prince<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="33" osisRef="Rev.1.5" osisID="Rev.1.5!note.33">This word, נגיד is used in Daniel 9:25-26, speaking of the Nagid Mashiach – Messiah the Prince.</note> commanding on the earth, the One who loves us and washes<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="34" osisRef="Rev.1.5" osisID="Rev.1.5!note.34">An unusual spelling רחצתנו and verb conjugation not found in the Tanakh, except in the simpler form used when Aaron and his sons were washed with water, readied by Moses to receive their priestly garments.</note> our transgressions<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="35" osisRef="Rev.1.5" osisID="Rev.1.5!note.35">Isaiah 53:5, the word, מעוונותינו means, “our transgression.”</note> in His blood.<verse eID="Rev.1.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.6" osisID="Rev.1.6" n="6"/>For he made us kings and priests <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="38" osisRef="Rev.1.6" osisID="Rev.1.6!note.38">Also see Exodus 19:6, Isa 61:6; 66:21; 1 Peter 2:5,9; Revelation 1:6, 5:10; 20:6.</note>before Yehovah, His Father. To Him be honor and glory forever and ever, amen.<verse eID="Rev.1.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.7" osisID="Rev.1.7" n="7"/>See, He will come in a cloud <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="40" osisRef="Rev.1.7" osisID="Rev.1.7!note.40">Daniel 7:13.</note>and those who pierced him will see Him. And all the clans <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="41" osisRef="Rev.1.7" osisID="Rev.1.7!note.41">Literally, “families or clans.” And the word for “tribe” is, ( שבט ) but in this case, I chose to use the word, “clans.”</note>of the earth will weep.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="42" osisRef="Rev.1.7" osisID="Rev.1.7!note.42">Zechariah 12:10.</note> Yes, Amen!<verse eID="Rev.1.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.8" osisID="Rev.1.8" n="8"/>I am He who is the First and the Last, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="45" osisRef="Rev.1.8" osisID="Rev.1.8!note.45">This is a direct quote from Isaiah 44:6, 48:12. See also Isaiah 41:4 and is a unique Hebrew marker. The “Alpha” and “Omega” are modifications in the Greek manuscripts.</note>the beginning and the end, says the Almighty, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="46" osisRef="Rev.1.8" osisID="Rev.1.8!note.46">Literally, “mighty Master.”</note>the one who is, and who was, and who will be.<verse eID="Rev.1.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.9" osisID="Rev.1.9" n="9"/>I, John, was on the island called Patmos for the sake of the Word of Yehovah and the testimony <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="48" osisRef="Rev.1.9" osisID="Rev.1.9!note.48">See Revelation 1:2 for more details on this word.</note>of Yeshua the Messiah.<verse eID="Rev.1.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.10" osisID="Rev.1.10" n="10"/>And the Holy Spirit soaked <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="51" osisRef="Rev.1.10" osisID="Rev.1.10!note.51">Jastrow and Klein indicate this word means “soaked” as used in the Talmud. However, it can also simply mean to “rest” or “dwell.” The successors to the high priesthood (Cohanim) were consecrated by profusely pouring oil over the Cohen Gadol’s (High Priest) heads, (see Leviticus 21:10-12; Exodus 29:7; Psalm 133:2). But for the remaining priest’s consecration it was the ritual blood that was applied to the ear, thumb, and big toe — but not oil (Exodus 29:20). However, the leper, on the day of his cleansing has a process to go through for cleansing, and the remaining oil… “he shall put on the head of him who is to be cleansed,” (Leviticus 14:25). Then after the application of the oil upon the ear, thumb, and toe of the restored leper, it was the applied in a more customary way of anointing the head of the restored leper as in the same manner the priests and kings were anointed (Leviticus 14:14-18).</note>over me, and I heard a great voice from behind me,<verse eID="Rev.1.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.11" osisID="Rev.1.11" n="11"/>then said, “I am the First and the Last;<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="53" osisRef="Rev.1.11" osisID="Rev.1.11!note.53">See verse the footnote in 1:8.</note> and all that you see, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="54" osisRef="Rev.1.11" osisID="Rev.1.11!note.54">Literally, “you saw” (past).</note>write in a scroll, and send it to the Elders of Asia; and to Ephesus, and to Smyrna, and to Pergamos, and to Thyatira, and to Sardis, and to Philadelphia, and to Laodicea.<verse eID="Rev.1.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.12" osisID="Rev.1.12" n="12"/>And I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. So, when I turned, I saw seven menorahs <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="57" osisRef="Rev.1.12" osisID="Rev.1.12!note.57">The word, מנורות (menorot) can refer to a menorah lamp stand of seven branches (see Zech 4), or it could refer to seven lampstands.</note>of gold.<verse eID="Rev.1.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.13" osisID="Rev.1.13" n="13"/>And in the midst of them was One in the likeness of man <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="58" osisRef="Rev.1.13" osisID="Rev.1.13!note.58">It is interesting to note that the Cochin manuscript doesn’t state that Yeshua is “one like the Son of man,” but in the likeness of man.</note>and dressed in white garments and girded upon the chest<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="59" osisRef="Rev.1.13" osisID="Rev.1.13!note.59">A chest binding can include the waist.</note> with a sash of gold.<verse eID="Rev.1.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.14" osisID="Rev.1.14" n="14"/>Indeed, his head and hair were white, as white as wool, and his eyes were as flames of fire.<verse eID="Rev.1.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.15" osisID="Rev.1.15" n="15"/>And his feet were like copper, and his voice was like the noise of mighty rushing waters.<verse eID="Rev.1.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.16" osisID="Rev.1.16" n="16"/>And seven stars were in his right hand, and from His mouth came a double-edged sword, and His appearance shined like the sun<verse eID="Rev.1.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.17" osisID="Rev.1.17" n="17"/>And when I saw Him, I fell, as if dead at His feet. And placing His right hand on me, He said, "Fear not. I am the First and the Last."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="62" osisRef="Rev.1.17" osisID="Rev.1.17!note.62">See the footnote in Revelation 1:8.</note><verse eID="Rev.1.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.18" osisID="Rev.1.18" n="18"/>“See I, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="63" osisRef="Rev.1.18" osisID="Rev.1.18!note.63">The tsere vowel marking under the alef of the first word of this verse emphasizes the imperative, “See!” The insertion of the nikkud “tsere” in verse 18 distinguishes the verb ראה from the past tense 3ms “he saw” to the imperative 2ms “see!”</note>I was dead, and now I live. I am eternal. And I have the keys to death and Gehinnom.”<verse eID="Rev.1.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.19" osisID="Rev.1.19" n="19"/>“Write what you saw, and what follows,”<verse eID="Rev.1.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.1.20" osisID="Rev.1.20" n="20"/>“and the mystery of the seven stars that you saw in His right hand, and the seven menorahs of gold. For the seven stars are the seven messengers, and the seven menorahs are the seven elders.”<verse eID="Rev.1.20"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.1"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.2" osisID="Rev.2" n="2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.1" osisID="Rev.2.1" n="1"/>“Then write to the messenger of the congregation of Ephesus. ‘He who has the seven stars in His right hand and walks among the seven menorahs of gold, says,’”<verse eID="Rev.2.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.2" osisID="Rev.2.2" n="2"/>“‘I know your works and hopes. You are not able to tolerate the wicked. You are testing those who say they are prophets,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="67" osisRef="Rev.2.2" osisID="Rev.2.2!note.67">Compare to Deuteronomy 13:1-5.</note> and finding they are liars.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.3" osisID="Rev.2.3" n="3"/>“’So, your hopes and your troubles are all for the sake of My Name.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.4" osisID="Rev.2.4" n="4"/>“’ So, I am bewildered. Why would you leave your first love?’”<verse eID="Rev.2.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.5" osisID="Rev.2.5" n="5"/>“‘Remember who you are and repent, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="74" osisRef="Rev.2.5" osisID="Rev.2.5!note.74">Literally, תשובה (teshuva) is a noun meaning, “Repent” and means, "do repentance," It is an idiom meaning to turn back to Yehovah and His ways. See Numbers 10:36.</note>and do your first works! But if not, your menorah will be quickly pushed from its place if you do not repent.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="75" osisRef="Rev.2.5" osisID="Rev.2.5!note.75">See footnotes on Revelation 2:7 regarding the end of day servants of Yehovah.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.6" osisID="Rev.2.6" n="6"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" osisRef="Rev.2.6" osisID="Rev.2.6!note.absent">This verse does not exist in the Cochin Oo.1.16.2 manuscript</note><verse eID="Rev.2.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.7" osisID="Rev.2.7" n="7" subType="x-alt-2.6"/>“’Whoever has ears, hears what the Spirit says to the congregation: Whoever prevails will eat from the Tree of Life,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="79" osisRef="Rev.2.7" osisID="Rev.2.7!note.79">This exact phrase in Hebrew is used in Genesis 3:22, where it is commonly translated as "Tree of Life;" however, "life" is plural, so it is literally, "the tree of lives" or "the living tree." This phrase is also found in Testaments of the Twelve Patriarchs 18:11, The Midrash of Philo 3:22:7.</note> which is in the Garden of Eden.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="80" osisRef="Rev.2.7" osisID="Rev.2.7!note.80">Joel 2: 1-2 describes a people in the day of Yehovah, a future day of great judgement as “a fire has consumed and behind them a fire burns” as they pursue the Garden of Eden. For in that day (the judgement day of Yehovah), a mighty people will arise, a great army of Yehovah, “…a people many and strong, the like of whom has never been, nor shall there ever be again after them, to the years of many generations.” This mighty army appears to be the servants of Yehovah who will do mighty deeds. Yehovah expects highly of His servants to repent and serve Yehovah in the greatest time and place in the history of the children of Israel.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.8" osisID="Rev.2.8" n="8" subType="x-alt-2.7"/>“Then write to the messenger of the congregation of Smyrna, ‘Thus says the First and the Last of the dead and is risen.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.9" osisID="Rev.2.9" n="9" subType="x-alt-2.8"/>“I know your works and sorrows, and the blasphemy of those who say they are Jews but are not. They are the children of Satan.”<verse eID="Rev.2.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.10" osisID="Rev.2.10" n="10" subType="x-alt-2.9"/>“Do not fear them. See, Satan will possess them in order to test you. And the sorrow will last for ten days. Be faithful until your death is set in motion, so that I may give to you the crown of life.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="86" osisRef="Rev.2.10" osisID="Rev.2.10!note.86">Literally, "the crown of lives." Also see footnotes on Revelation 2:7 regarding the end of day servants of Yehovah.</note> “Do not fear them. See, Satan will take some among the captives <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="87" osisRef="Rev.2.10" osisID="Rev.2.10!note.87">This word, בשביה (ba’shiv’yah) is used in Deuteronomy 21:11, 2 Chronicles 28:15, and Jeremiah 48:46 and is translated as “among the captives.”</note>in order to test you. And the sorrow will last for ten days. May you be faithful until your death, so that I may give to you the crown of life.”<verse eID="Rev.2.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.11" osisID="Rev.2.11" n="11" subType="x-alt-2.10"/>“Whoever has ears, hears <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="88" osisRef="Rev.2.11" osisID="Rev.2.11!note.88">“Ears to hear” is first found in Deuteronomy 29:4.</note>what the Spirit is speaking to the congregation: and whoever that prevails will not have the anguish of another death.”<verse eID="Rev.2.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.12" osisID="Rev.2.12" n="12" subType="x-alt-2.11"/>“Write to the messenger of the congregation of Pergamum: ‘Thus, says the One who has the double- edged sword,’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="89" osisRef="Rev.2.12" osisID="Rev.2.12!note.89">The Hebrew idiom “two-edged sword” is first found in Psalms 149:6.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.13" osisID="Rev.2.13" n="13" subType="x-alt-2.12"/>“’I know your works and your place. You are in the place of the throne of Satan. Believe in My Name,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="91" osisRef="Rev.2.13" osisID="Rev.2.13!note.91">Compare to Joel 2:32, Psalms 91:14-16, 99:6 and Exodus 34:6-7 when Yehovah called out His own Name before revealing His glory to Moses. The power of deliverance, healing and the revealing of Yehovah’s glory is through calling out His Name.</note> and my faithfulness, and my timing. He killed my faithful friend, Antipas, who walked with you in the place where Satan dwells.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.14" osisID="Rev.2.14" n="14" subType="x-alt-2.13"/>“‘And you also hate those among you who are students of the teachings of Balaam, who guided the way of Balak to do evil to the children of Israel, causing them to sin by whoredom.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="94" osisRef="Rev.2.14" osisID="Rev.2.14!note.94">Balaam advised the Moabites how to entice the children of Israel with prostitutes and idolatry. He could not curse Israel directly, so he devised a plan for Israel to bring a curse upon themselves. Balak followed Balaam’s advice, and Israel fell into sin, worshiping Baal of Peor and committing fornication with Midianite women. And in Numbers 31:16, the Midianite women were kept alive when Moses sent one thousand from each tribe to battle with the Midianites and all the Midianite women who had laid with a man was ordered to be killed. See Numbers chapter 22, 23, and 31:16; Deuteronomy 23:4-5; Joshua 13:22 for more details. Also consider that “stubbornness” is described as “idolatry” and “rebellion” as “witchcraft,” in 1 Samuel 15:23.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.15" osisID="Rev.2.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-2.14"/>“’But also, you hate the teachings of the Nicolaitans, which also I hate of these.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.16" osisID="Rev.2.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-2.15"/>“Repent! <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="99" osisRef="Rev.2.16" osisID="Rev.2.16!note.99">Literally, “do repentance.” Also see footnotes on Revelation 2:7 regarding the end of day servants of Yehovah.</note>But if you do not, I will come swiftly to make war with you, by the sword in my mouth.”<verse eID="Rev.2.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.17" osisID="Rev.2.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-2.16"/>“’Whoever has ears, hear whatever the Spirit says to the congregation: Whoever prevails, I want to give him the hidden manna to eat;<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="102" osisRef="Rev.2.17" osisID="Rev.2.17!note.102">The hidden manna seems to be referencing Yeshua. In John 6:35, Y eshua says that He is the manna that was sent from heaven by Yehovah. And in John 6:55, Yeshua said, “For My flesh is truly food, and My blood is truly dink.” But 6:35 gives the answer how to partake of Yeshua, for He says, “…He who comes to Me shall not get hungry at all, and he who believes in Me shall not get thirsty at all.” (emphasis mine). What does “coming to Yeshua” mean? Perhaps it means coming in prayer, praise, worship, studying the Word of Yehovah and blowing the shofar according Nehemia 4:19-20; then there will be no hunger nor thirst.</note> but also, good testimonies of him a̶nd in the testimonies, a new name written so that not anyone can read except he who receives it.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.18" osisID="Rev.2.18" n="18" subType="x-alt-2.17"/>“And to the messenger of the congregation of Thyatira write, ‘Thus says the Son of Yehovah, whose eyes are as flames of fire, his legs, copper,’”<verse eID="Rev.2.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.19" osisID="Rev.2.19" n="19" subType="x-alt-2.18"/>“’I know your works - your love, your service, your faith, your hope - in all seasons, doing more.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.20" osisID="Rev.2.20" n="20" subType="x-alt-2.19"/>“But I have a small matter - You must abandon the woman Jezebel who says she is a prophetess but teaches and inflames to cause my servants to commit whoredom.”<verse eID="Rev.2.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.22" osisID="Rev.2.22" n="22" subType="x-alt-2.20" type="x-reordered"/>“’See I, I bring great sorrow upon her and those who lay <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="106" osisRef="Rev.2.22" osisID="Rev.2.22!note.106">Literally, “lays with her.”</note>with her, if they do not repent of their works.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.22"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.21" osisID="Rev.2.21" n="21" subType="x-alt-2.21" type="x-reordered"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" osisRef="Rev.2.21" osisID="Rev.2.21!note.order">The Cochin manuscript changes the order of the following verses.</note>‘” For I gave her time to repent.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="107" osisRef="Rev.2.21" osisID="Rev.2.21!note.107">Literally, “to do repentance.”</note><verse eID="Rev.2.21"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.23" osisID="Rev.2.23" n="23" subType="x-alt-2.22"/>“’And her children will be put to death: And all the congregation will know it is I who is the tester the innermost parts.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="108" osisRef="Rev.2.23" osisID="Rev.2.23!note.108">Literally, “the kidneys and the heart,” which is a Hebrew idiom for a person’s (nefesh) meaning, “soul”.</note> And everyone will be paid according to his measure.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.23"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.24" osisID="Rev.2.24" n="24" subType="x-alt-2.23"/>“’However, to you and others in Thyatira, I say that I will not bring more sorrow upon you because you do not learn the teachings of Satan.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.24"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.25" osisID="Rev.2.25" n="25" subType="x-alt-2.24"/>“’However, be strong in what you have, until I come.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="109" osisRef="Rev.2.25" osisID="Rev.2.25!note.109">This is likely a contraction of the phrase עד שאני בא which means "until I come.”</note><verse eID="Rev.2.25"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.26" osisID="Rev.2.26" n="26" subType="x-alt-2.25"/>“’But to him that prevails, I will give the nations for his inheritance!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="110" osisRef="Rev.2.26" osisID="Rev.2.26!note.110">Verse 26 is likely a paraphrase of Psalm 2:7-8, because verse 27 alludes to Psalm 2:9.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.26"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.27a" osisID="Rev.2.27a" n="27a" subType="x-alt-2.26"/>“’And He leads them with a rod of iron, as an instrument <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="111" osisRef="Rev.2.27a" osisID="Rev.2.27a!note.111">See Psalms 2:9, Jewish Publication Society (JPS). This phrase is also used in Revelation 12:5, and 19:15.</note>that shatters them into pieces.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="112" osisRef="Rev.2.27a" osisID="Rev.2.27a!note.112">See Daniel 2:44, “And in the days of these kings shall the God of heaven set up a kingdom, which shall never be destroyed: and the kingdom shall not be left to other people, but it shall break in pieces and consume all these kingdoms, and it shall stand forever.”</note><verse eID="Rev.2.27a"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.27b" osisID="Rev.2.27b" n="27b" subType="x-alt-2.27"/>“‘As I have received from my Father, so I will give to him the dawn.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="114" osisRef="Rev.2.27b" osisID="Rev.2.27b!note.114">“The dawn” or “the sunrise.”</note><verse eID="Rev.2.27b"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.28" osisID="Rev.2.28" n="28"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" osisRef="Rev.2.28" osisID="Rev.2.28!note.absent">This verse does not exist in the Cochin manuscript.</note><verse eID="Rev.2.28"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.2.29" osisID="Rev.2.29" n="29" subType="x-alt-2.28"/>“’Whoever has ears, hears what the Spirit says to the congregation.’”<verse eID="Rev.2.29"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.2"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.3" osisID="Rev.3" n="3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.1" osisID="Rev.3.1" n="1"/>“Write to the messenger of the congregation of Sardis: ‘Thus says the one who has the Spirits of Yehovah<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="115" osisRef="Rev.3.1" osisID="Rev.3.1!note.115">See Revelation 1:4 for more details on the seven Spirits of Yehovah.</note> and the seven stars: I know your works because you have a name<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="116" osisRef="Rev.3.1" osisID="Rev.3.1!note.116">The word, שם (shem) means “name” or can mean “reputation.”</note> that lives, yet you are dead.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.2" osisID="Rev.3.2" n="2"/>“’Therefore, do not sleep, but be strong for those who want to die, because I did not find your works before Yehovah completed.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.3" osisID="Rev.3.3" n="3"/>“‘And now remember how you received and heard and establish them. So, repent! <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="117" osisRef="Rev.3.3" osisID="Rev.3.3!note.117">Literally, “you (feminine singular) make repentance!” Also see footnotes on Revelation 2:7 regarding the end of day servants of Yehovah.</note>But if not, I will come upon you unexpectedly,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="118" osisRef="Rev.3.3" osisID="Rev.3.3!note.118">“As a thief” is an idiom meaning “unexpectedly,” as in 1 Thessalonians 5:2, 2 Peter 3:10.</note> and you do not know which hour I come.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.4" osisID="Rev.3.4" n="4"/>“Furthermore, your name is heard in Sardis. For they are worthy because they did not defile their garments<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="121" osisRef="Rev.3.4" osisID="Rev.3.4!note.121">“They did not defile their garments,” meaning, “they did not defile themselves.”</note> and will walk with me in white robes.”<verse eID="Rev.3.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.5" osisID="Rev.3.5" n="5"/>“Whoever that prevails will walk in white robes, and I will not blot out his name from the Book of Life and I will declare his name before my Father, and before His messengers.”<verse eID="Rev.3.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.6" osisID="Rev.3.6" n="6"/>“’Whoever has ears, hears what the Spirit says to the congregation.”<verse eID="Rev.3.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.7" osisID="Rev.3.7" n="7"/>“To the messenger of the congregation of Philadelphia, write: ‘Thus says the Holy and the Faithful. This is he who has the Key of David, the One who opens, and no one can shut; and the One<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="123" osisRef="Rev.3.7" osisID="Rev.3.7!note.123">The word אחד (echad), “one” can be translated as meaning, a person.</note> who shuts, so no man can open,’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="124" osisRef="Rev.3.7" osisID="Rev.3.7!note.124">See Isaiah 22:22.</note><verse eID="Rev.3.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.8" osisID="Rev.3.8" n="8"/>“’I know your works. See, I have given you an open doorway, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="126" osisRef="Rev.3.8" osisID="Rev.3.8!note.126">This phrase can mean, “door, opening, entrance, or opportunity” and is similar to John 10:9, when Yeshua says he is הפתח “the door.” It is also interesting to note that Psalms 24 speaks of an “everlasting doorway” at Har Yehovah (i.e. Mountain of Yehovah, or Mount Sinai). The language seems to be describing some kind of portal.</note>that no one can shut. But you have little power, but you kept my Words and did not deny My Name.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.9" osisID="Rev.3.9" n="9"/>“See, I will give those of the fellowship of Satan <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="128" osisRef="Rev.3.9" osisID="Rev.3.9!note.128">Or “Fellowship of the accuser.”</note>who say they are Jews, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="129" osisRef="Rev.3.9" osisID="Rev.3.9!note.129">The word, “Yehudim,” can mean “Judean.”</note>but are not t̶hey are only liars. See, I will make them come to pray<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="130" osisRef="Rev.3.9" osisID="Rev.3.9!note.130">Root: פלל meaning, “to intervene, interpose, pray.” In the Pi’el binyan verb stem means, “to mediate, judge, execute judgement.” This word is misspelled and should be: לְפַלֵל (lefalel).</note> at your feet, for you to know that I love you.’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="131" osisRef="Rev.3.9" osisID="Rev.3.9!note.131">See Isaiah 45:14, 49:23, 60:14.</note><verse eID="Rev.3.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.10" osisID="Rev.3.10" n="10"/>“Because you kept <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="132" osisRef="Rev.3.10" osisID="Rev.3.10!note.132">Literally, “guarded.”</note>the Word, as I hoped, I will keep you from the time of the coming trial upon the earth, to test those dwelling upon the earth.”<verse eID="Rev.3.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.11" osisID="Rev.3.11" n="11"/>“See, I will come quickly. Guard what you have, so that no one will take your crown.”<verse eID="Rev.3.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.12" osisID="Rev.3.12" n="12"/>“Whoever prevails, I will write on him the name of my Eloah, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="135" osisRef="Rev.3.12" osisID="Rev.3.12!note.135">This word is repeated three times in this verse and is the singular form of Elohim.</note>and the name of the new Jerusalem, which is the city of my Eloah, which will come from the heavens to the earth, and also My new Name.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.13" osisID="Rev.3.13" n="13"/>“’Whoever who has ears, hears what the Spirit says to the congregation.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.14" osisID="Rev.3.14" n="14"/>“And to the messenger of the congregation of Laodicea said, “Write, ‘Amen! <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="137" osisRef="Rev.3.14" osisID="Rev.3.14!note.137">“Amen” אמן is from the root of emet “truth.”.</note>The faithful witness from the beginning of creation.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.15" osisID="Rev.3.15" n="15"/>“’I know your works, that you are neither cold nor hot. I would rather that you be either cold or hot!’”<verse eID="Rev.3.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.16" osisID="Rev.3.16" n="16"/>“However, since you are neither cold nor hot, for this reason I will spit you out of my mouth.”<verse eID="Rev.3.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.17" osisID="Rev.3.17" n="17"/>“And you will say, ‘I am wealthy, and I have enough and have no need of anything.’ However, you do not know that you are poor and destitute!’”<verse eID="Rev.3.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.18" osisID="Rev.3.18" n="18"/>“’I give my counsel to you: Acquire wealth <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="140" osisRef="Rev.3.18" osisID="Rev.3.18!note.140">Literally, “gold.”</note>from Me. And you will dress in white garments, and I will anoint your eyes with oil, so that you will be able to see.’”<verse eID="Rev.3.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.19" osisID="Rev.3.19" n="19"/>“’I send a message to those whom I loved. So now, repent!’”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="142" osisRef="Rev.3.19" osisID="Rev.3.19!note.142">Literally, “do repentance.” Also see footnotes on Revelation 2:7 regarding the end of day servants of Yehovah.</note><verse eID="Rev.3.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.20" osisID="Rev.3.20" n="20"/>“See! I am standing before the door knocking. If one hears my voice and opens the door, I will walk in.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="144" osisRef="Rev.3.20" osisID="Rev.3.20!note.144">The word שׁמע (shema) is the verb “to hear, to hearken, to listen, to obey” and is found in Deuteronomy 6:4.</note><verse eID="Rev.3.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.21" osisID="Rev.3.21" n="21"/>“Whomever prevails, I will permit him to sit with Me upon My throne, in the same way as I have overcome and sit with my Father upon His throne.”<verse eID="Rev.3.21"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.3.22" osisID="Rev.3.22" n="22"/>“Whoever who has ears, hears what the Spirit says.”<verse eID="Rev.3.22"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.3"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.4" osisID="Rev.4" n="4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.1" osisID="Rev.4.1" n="1"/>Then then I saw an open door in the heavens, and the first voice I heard speaking to me was like the voice of a shofar.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="145" osisRef="Rev.4.1" osisID="Rev.4.1!note.145">The voice of a shofar implies the shofar blast of war (see Nehemiah 4:20). For after the shofar blast, the Spirit soaks over John and he is shown various images in the heavenlies that demonstrates Yehovah and His Seven Spirits are about to bring judgement. And, in Revelation 4:8, Yehovah is called, “Yehovah of Armies.” Then, in Revelation 5, the seals are opened and Yehovah brings a great judgement upon the earth. It is also important to note that in Nehemiah 4:20, at the sound of the shofar blast, Yehovah fights on behalf of the children of Israel.</note> It said, “Come here, I want to show you what comes after this!”<verse eID="Rev.4.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.2" osisID="Rev.4.2" n="2"/>And immediately, the Holy Spirit soaked over me, and I saw a throne sitting in the heavens, and upon the throne was One sitting upon it.<verse eID="Rev.4.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.3" osisID="Rev.4.3" n="3"/>Then He who sat upon it, His countenance was like the appearance of a sapphire stone and jasper, and around the throne was a rainbow as an emerald.<verse eID="Rev.4.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.4" osisID="Rev.4.4" n="4"/>And around the throne were twenty-four seats. Upon them were sitting twenty-four elders, clothed in white garments, and upon their heads, a crown of gold.<verse eID="Rev.4.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.5" osisID="Rev.4.5" n="5"/>Then from the throne came sounds - thunders and lightnings - and seven lamps were before the throne for those are the seven Spirits of Yehovah.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="146" osisRef="Rev.4.5" osisID="Rev.4.5!note.146">See Revelation 1:4 for more details on the seven Spirits of Yehovah.</note><verse eID="Rev.4.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.6" osisID="Rev.4.6" n="6"/>And before the throne was a sea of glass <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="147" osisRef="Rev.4.6" osisID="Rev.4.6!note.147">Or “sea of glass or reflecting pool.”</note>like an amethyst; and in the midst and around the throne were four living creatures<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="148" osisRef="Rev.4.6" osisID="Rev.4.6!note.148">The word חיה can mean “beast” or “living creature.” But when referring to the beast of Satan, “beast” is used and “living creature” when referring to the creatures before the throne of Yehovah.</note> full of eyes in front and back.<verse eID="Rev.4.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.7" osisID="Rev.4.7" n="7"/>And the first living creature was like the image of a lion; the second like an ox; the third like a man; and the fourth, like an eagle.<verse eID="Rev.4.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.8" osisID="Rev.4.8" n="8"/>Every one of them had six wings <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="150" osisRef="Rev.4.8" osisID="Rev.4.8!note.150">See Isaiah 6:3.</note>and filled with eyes within. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="151" osisRef="Rev.4.8" osisID="Rev.4.8!note.151">See Ezekiel 1:18.</note>And there was no rest for them day or night, as they continually declare, “Holy, Holy, Holy<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="152" osisRef="Rev.4.8" osisID="Rev.4.8!note.152">See Isaiah 6:1-13.</note> is Yehovah of Armies, the Almighty One, who was, is, and will be!”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="153" osisRef="Rev.4.8" osisID="Rev.4.8!note.153">See Exodus 3:14.</note><verse eID="Rev.4.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.9" osisID="Rev.4.9" n="9"/>At that time, the living creatures praised and gave thanks to this One sitting upon the throne and lives forever and ever.<verse eID="Rev.4.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.10" osisID="Rev.4.10" n="10"/>There were twenty-four elders prostrate before Him praying <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="154" osisRef="Rev.4.10" osisID="Rev.4.10!note.154">Root: פלל meaning, “to intervene, interpose, pray.” In the Pi’el binyan verb stem means, “to mediate, judge, execute judgement” (See Revelation 3:9). The Hit’palel verb binyan stem can be either reflexive or reciprocal. The Hit’palel is usually (1) reflexive (doing this to oneself). (2) or it is reciprocal as in a dialogue. The Hit’palel word הִתְפַלֵל from Arabic لَّفَ (falla) means, “notch edge” (of sword, etc.), i.e. “cut oneself in worship.” Therefore, it seems to indicate one who inflicts himself to intercede or to execute a judgement from Elohim (God). The Hit’palel stem verb binyan is found in 1 Kings 8:42, 1 Samuel 1:27; 3:26; 5:3; Numbers 11:2; 21:7; Ezekiel 10:1; Jeremiah 29:7, 42:4; Genesis 20:7, 17; Deuteronomy 9:20, 25; 1 Samuel 2:25, 7:5; 12; 2 Chronicles 30:18; and Job 42:8 (late). Lastly, linguistic anthropology is very difficult when defining the meaning of this verb when it was used. However, consider a few options to the meaning of this word. (1) It can mean “Inflicting himself to bring an executed judgement.” (2) It can also mean dialoging (with Yehovah). (3) Or perhaps it means both: “Praying/inflicting oneself for a judgement while dialoging.”</note>the One who lives forever and ever, casting their crowns before Him, saying, The scriptures: the twenty-four elders fall down before Him who sits on the throne and bow before Him who lives forever and ever, and they cast their crowns before the throne, saying,<verse eID="Rev.4.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.4.11" osisID="Rev.4.11" n="11"/>“Lord, You are worthy to receive honor and glory. For You created all things, and by the hand of Your will, everything was made."<verse eID="Rev.4.11"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.4"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.5" osisID="Rev.5" n="5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.1" osisID="Rev.5.1" n="1"/>And the One who sat upon the throne: I saw in His right hand a scroll sealed from within and on the outside with seven seals.<verse eID="Rev.5.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.2" osisID="Rev.5.2" n="2"/>And I saw a messenger cry out with a great voice, “Who is worthy to open the scroll and break its seals?”<verse eID="Rev.5.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.3" osisID="Rev.5.3" n="3"/>But no one in the heavens or on the earth was able to open the scroll and see it.<verse eID="Rev.5.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.4" osisID="Rev.5.4" n="4"/>And I wept much because not one was found worthy to do this thing.<verse eID="Rev.5.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.5" osisID="Rev.5.5" n="5"/>Then one of the elders said to me, “Do not weep! See, the eternal Lion from the family of Judah, the root of David, has prevailed to open the scroll and its seals!”<verse eID="Rev.5.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.6" osisID="Rev.5.6" n="6"/>I saw in the midst of the throne, the living creatures and the twenty-four elders, standing as a Lamb that was killed. And He had seven horns and seven eyes, which are the seven Spirits of Yehovah<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="157" osisRef="Rev.5.6" osisID="Rev.5.6!note.157">See Isaiah 11:2 for the use of יהוה and not אלהים . Also refer to Revelation 1:4 for more details on the seven Spirits of Yehovah.</note> sent into all the earth.<verse eID="Rev.5.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.7" osisID="Rev.5.7" n="7"/>And He came and took the scroll from the right hand of He who sat upon the throne.<verse eID="Rev.5.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.8" osisID="Rev.5.8" n="8"/>And when He took the scroll, the four living creatures <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="158" osisRef="Rev.5.8" osisID="Rev.5.8!note.158">Ezekiel 1:5-6.</note>and twenty-four elders fell before the Lamb. And in their hands were a lyre and censors full of incense, which are the prayers of the holy ones.<verse eID="Rev.5.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.9" osisID="Rev.5.9" n="9"/>And they sang <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="159" osisRef="Rev.5.9" osisID="Rev.5.9!note.159">This can also be qatal, past tense. The (root: זמר ) means “song, might, and plucking. Hebrew and Aramaic Lexicon of the Old Testament (HALOT). According to Cohanim (Priests) in Israel, this word means the “Power of God in the music.” David is an example of a miracle singer (1 Samuel 16:18, 23; 2 Samuel 23:1). There are 57 miracle songs in the Psalms called מִזְמֹור (mizmorim – plural). The root is זָּׁמַר (zamar). Suzanne Haïk-Vantoura developed a music key of the ta’amim (i.e. cantillation markings) and developed the music of the Tanakh. She successfully translated all 150 Psalms into the music of the Bible. Bob McDonald, a musician and computer software programmer created a software to translate the t’amim according to Suzanne’s key and was able to translate much of the Tanakh into music. See website: https://meafar.blogspot.com/p/about.html.</note>a renewed song and said, “You are worthy to take the scroll and open its seals, for You were slain, and You redeemed us by Your own blood.”<verse eID="Rev.5.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.10" osisID="Rev.5.10" n="10"/>“You made us to be priests and kings <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="162" osisRef="Rev.5.10" osisID="Rev.5.10!note.162">Also see Exodus 19:6, Isa 61:6; 66:21; 1 Peter 2:5,9; Revelation 1:6, 5:10; 20:6.</note>so we become kings upon the earth.”<verse eID="Rev.5.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.11" osisID="Rev.5.11" n="11"/>Then I saw and heard many voices of messengers surrounding the throne. Their number was thousands of thousands.<verse eID="Rev.5.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.12" osisID="Rev.5.12" n="12"/>Then they say with a loud voice, “The Lamb that was killed is worthy of power, honor, glory, and prayers!”<verse eID="Rev.5.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.13" osisID="Rev.5.13" n="13"/>All the living creatures in the heavens and upon the earth, beneath the earth and in the sea: I heard them say, “To the One sitting upon the throne and to the Lamb, are given glory, adoration, and praise forever and ever!”<verse eID="Rev.5.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.5.14" osisID="Rev.5.14" n="14"/>Then the four living creatures answer, “Amen!” And the twenty-four elders fell, praying to the One who lives forever and ever.<verse eID="Rev.5.14"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.5"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.6" osisID="Rev.6" n="6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.1" osisID="Rev.6.1" n="1"/>And I saw the Lamb open one of the seals and I heard the four living creatures say as one voice, “Come and see!”<verse eID="Rev.6.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.2" osisID="Rev.6.2" n="2"/>Then I saw a white horse, and he who sat upon him had a bow in his hand, and a crown given to him. And he proceeded to prevail and be victorious.<verse eID="Rev.6.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.3" osisID="Rev.6.3" n="3"/>And when the second seal was opened. I heard the second living creature say, “Come and see!”<verse eID="Rev.6.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.4" osisID="Rev.6.4" n="4"/>Then another went out; a red horse. Sitting on it was authorized to take peace from the earth, and he was given a great sword.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="165" osisRef="Rev.6.4" osisID="Rev.6.4!note.165">This phrase can be translated as, “he was granted to do great destruction.”</note><verse eID="Rev.6.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.5" osisID="Rev.6.5" n="5"/>Then when the third seal was opened, the third living creature said, “Come and see!” And I saw a black horse and one sitting on it had a set of scales in his hand.<verse eID="Rev.6.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.6" osisID="Rev.6.6" n="6"/>Then I heard a voice in the midst of the four living creatures say, “A measure of wheat for two faces,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="167" osisRef="Rev.6.6" osisID="Rev.6.6!note.167">Possible Hebrew idiom meaning, “faces of coins” or “coins.”</note> three measures of barley for two faces; but the oil and the wine do not injure.<verse eID="Rev.6.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.7" osisID="Rev.6.7" n="7"/>And when the fourth seal opened, I heard the fourth living creature say, “Come and see!”<verse eID="Rev.6.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.8" osisID="Rev.6.8" n="8"/>Then I saw a strong mottled <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="168" osisRef="Rev.6.8" osisID="Rev.6.8!note.168">Can also mean, “speckled.” See Genesis 30:10, Exodus 9:18, 28:17, 30:30. This appears to have a direct correlation with the horses of Zechariah 6.</note>horse and the one who sat on him was named the Messenger of Death, and Gehinnom<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="169" osisRef="Rev.6.8" osisID="Rev.6.8!note.169">Gehinnom is a valley outside of Jerusalem where Solomon built a statue of Moloch.</note> went after him. He was given authority to cause death to a fourth upon the earth with sword, hunger, and death by the living creatures of the earth.<verse eID="Rev.6.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.9" osisID="Rev.6.9" n="9"/>And when He opened the fifth, I saw beneath <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="171" osisRef="Rev.6.9" osisID="Rev.6.9!note.171">According to Jastrow’ s Dictionary, this word תחת (tachat) also means, “at the foot,” as compared to Exodus 24:4 and Deuteronomy 4:11.</note>the holy place, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="172" osisRef="Rev.6.9" osisID="Rev.6.9!note.172">The word, ההיכל (ha’hekal) can mean, “the Temple” or “the holy place.”</note>the souls that were killed for the Holy Name, and for the sake of their testimony.<verse eID="Rev.6.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.10" osisID="Rev.6.10" n="10"/>Then they shout with a great voice saying, “The Lord is holy and faithful! Until when will You judge, and avenge our blood from those dwelling upon the earth?”<verse eID="Rev.6.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.11" osisID="Rev.6.11" n="11"/>And every one of them was given white garments, and it was said to them, “Relax still, a little while.”<verse eID="Rev.6.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.12" osisID="Rev.6.12" n="12"/>Then I saw that He opened the sixth then there were tremblings of the earth, and the sun became dark, and the moon was red as blood.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="177" osisRef="Rev.6.12" osisID="Rev.6.12!note.177">The events to follow will be dreadful. But those who know and call upon the Name of Yehovah shall be delivered. These events appear be described in Joel 2:30-32 as, “And I shall give signs in the heavens and upon the earth: blood and fire and columns of smoke, the sun is turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the coming of the great and awesome day of יהוה (Yehovah). And it shall be that everyone who calls on the Name of יהוה shall be delivered. For on Mount Tsiyon and in Yerushalayim there shall be an escape as יהוה (Yehovah) has said, and among the survivors whom יהוה (Yehovah) calls.” The Scriptures 2009, (with Yehovah’s Name added in English). Also see Psalms 91:14-16.</note><verse eID="Rev.6.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.13" osisID="Rev.6.13" n="13"/>All their armies drop as a withering leaf from the vine, so like a withering fig tree.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="181" osisRef="Rev.6.13" osisID="Rev.6.13!note.181">This verse is a stunning reflection of Isaiah 34:4, The "Revised Jewish Public Society 2023" version of Isaiah 34:4 translates as, “The heavens shall be rolled up like a scroll, and all their host shall wither Like a leaf withering on the vine, Or shriveled fruit on a fig tree.”</note><verse eID="Rev.6.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.14" osisID="Rev.6.14" n="14"/>Then all the mountains and hills he moved to and fro, and they were shaken <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="183" osisRef="Rev.6.14" osisID="Rev.6.14!note.183">This word is oddly spelled. It appears the word, נד (nod) should have been a separate word meaning, “he moved to and fro.” The second half of the word is also misspelled. It is possible that he meant, זעזעו (zu’az’zu)” they were shaken” and with the preceding word נד it would read as, “he moved to and fro and they were shaken.” Or it could be that the final three letters of the word נדעזו could read as, זעו - “they moved.” If this is two words, it could translate to, “he moved them to and fro.”</note>from their places.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="184" osisRef="Rev.6.14" osisID="Rev.6.14!note.184">Compare to Isaiah 22:15.</note><verse eID="Rev.6.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.15" osisID="Rev.6.15" n="15"/>And the kings of the earth, the rulers, the wealthy, the officers, the servants, and the freemen hid themselves in rocks, in caves, and in the burrows of the earth<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="186" osisRef="Rev.6.15" osisID="Rev.6.15!note.186">Compare to Isaiah 2:19.</note><verse eID="Rev.6.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.16" osisID="Rev.6.16" n="16"/>saying to the mountains and to the rocks, “Fall on us, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="188" osisRef="Rev.6.16" osisID="Rev.6.16!note.188">Hosea 10:8.</note>and hide us from before the sight of Him sitting upon the throne<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="189" osisRef="Rev.6.16" osisID="Rev.6.16!note.189">Compare to Luke 23:30.</note> who sits upon the throne, and from the wrath of the Lamb.”<verse eID="Rev.6.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.6.17" osisID="Rev.6.17" n="17"/>“For the day of His wrath of anger has come, and who will be able to stand before Him?”<verse eID="Rev.6.17"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.6"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.7" osisID="Rev.7" n="7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.1" osisID="Rev.7.1" n="1"/>Then after this, I saw four messengers standing upon the four corners of the earth, gripping the four winds<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="190" osisRef="Rev.7.1" osisID="Rev.7.1!note.190">See Jeremiah 49:36 describing the “four winds.”</note> of the earth in their hands, so that the wind would not be upon the earth nor upon the waters, nor on any tree.<verse eID="Rev.7.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.2" osisID="Rev.7.2" n="2"/>And I saw another messenger who came from the east of the sun. With him was the seal of the living and existing El. He cried out with a great voice to the four messengers who were permitted to damage the earth and the sea.<verse eID="Rev.7.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.3" osisID="Rev.7.3" n="3"/>He said, “Don’t damage the earth, nor the sea, nor even a tree, until I give a mark (tav) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="192" osisRef="Rev.7.3" osisID="Rev.7.3!note.192">According to Jastrow Dictionary, this can also mean, “music,” or “music note.” It appears with the giving of the “tav” may bring a special type of music or frequency at their sealing. Also consider this may be the later day great outpouring described in Joel 2 and Acts 2. In Michael Rood’s video series called, Revelation The Fifth Gospel: Episode 7, he argues that the great outpouring of the Holy Spirit will occur on the 144,000 on the last great day of Sukkot. “144,000 Israelites who have been scattered for nearly two millennia will be sealed for divine protection before the nuclear brimstone hits the fan. They are the ones who will receive the latter rain outpouring, the double portion of the Holy Spirit spoken by the prophet Joel. That greatly anticipated prophetic event occurs at the conclusion of the war that brings Israel to the brink of destruction. “…The 144,000 of Israel will know His name. They will call on His name. They will be sent forth from Jerusalem to teach His name to all nations and peoples and kindreds and tongues, just as Israel was commanded to do.” See also Revelation 1:4 for more details.</note>upon the foreheads<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="193" osisRef="Rev.7.3" osisID="Rev.7.3!note.193">Compare to Ezekiel 9:4, “ תָּׁו עַל־מִצְחֹות ,” (tav al mitzchot, “tav upon the foreheads”) that seems to closely align this sealing of the 144,000 to the sealing described in Ezekiel 9.</note> of the servants<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="194" osisRef="Rev.7.3" osisID="Rev.7.3!note.194">“Servants” can be both men, women and possibly children sealed of the 144,000 for Yehovah also has female servants as described in Joel 2:28-29, “And after this it shall be that I pour out My Spirit on all flesh: And your sons and your daughters shall prophesy, your old men dream dreams, your young men see visions. And also on the male servants and on the female servants I shall pour out My Spirit in those days,” Next, consider in Ezekiel 9 that it appears the sealing may have also been men, women, and possibly children for the word, הָּׁאֲנָּׁשִׁים (ha’anashim) can be gender neural (i.e. common plural) meaning, “men, women, and children,” or rather, “mankind.” It is also interesting to note that Merrilyn Mansfield from The University of Sydney, Australia presented a strong argument that the women who served at the door of the tabernacle in Exodus 38:8 and 1 Samuel 2:22, did so in a Levitical capacity. See The Ministering Women and their Mirrors, July 6, 2011. This paper can be found on Academia.edu.</note> of Yehovah.”<verse eID="Rev.7.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.4" osisID="Rev.7.4" n="4"/>Then I heard the number that was sealed. One hundred forty-four thousand were sealed of the clans<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="196" osisRef="Rev.7.4" osisID="Rev.7.4!note.196">Literally, “families or clans.” And the word for “tribe” is, ( שבט ) but in this case, I chose to use the word, “clans.”</note> of the children of Israel:<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="197" osisRef="Rev.7.4" osisID="Rev.7.4!note.197">It is important to note this is not “Jews only” as some Christian doctrines claim. The children of Israel include all the lost tribes of Israel and the foreigner who joins himself to Yehovah and becomes part of Israel. Consider numerous examples of foreigners who became part of Israel, including the new covenant: Genesis 14, 24, Exodus 2:16-21; 12:38; 30:33, 38, 31:14; Leviticus 7:20, 21, 25, 27; Numbers 32:12; Joshua 2:1-2; Ruth 4:13-16; 2 Samuel 11:3; 1 Chronicles 18:17; 22:2; 2 Chronicles 32:25; Isaiah e5e6:6-7; Jeremiah 31:31-33; 35; Ester 8:17; 9:27; Matthew 26:28; Mark 11:17 14;24; Luke 22:20; John 20:20; Acts 2:9-11; 13:16, 26, 16:14, 17:17; 1 Corinthians 11:25, 2 Corinthians 3:6, Hebrews 8:6-13, 9:15, 10:16, 29, 12:24.</note><verse eID="Rev.7.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.5" osisID="Rev.7.5" n="5"/>From the tribe of Judah were twelve thousand; from the tribe of Reuben were twelve thousand; from the tribe of Gad were twelve thousand.<verse eID="Rev.7.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.6" osisID="Rev.7.6" n="6"/>From the tribe of Asher were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Naphtali were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Manasseh were twelve thousand.<verse eID="Rev.7.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.7" osisID="Rev.7.7" n="7"/>From the tribe of Simeon were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Levi were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Issachar were twelve thousand.<verse eID="Rev.7.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.8" osisID="Rev.7.8" n="8"/>From the tribe of Zebulun were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Joseph were twelve thousand. From the tribe of Benjamin were twelve thousand.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="198" osisRef="Rev.7.8" osisID="Rev.7.8!note.198">The tribe of Dan is not mentioned. See Genesis 49:17 and Judges 18:303-31 for more details.</note><verse eID="Rev.7.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.9" osisID="Rev.7.9" n="9"/>Then after this, I saw a great multitude that no one <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="199" osisRef="Rev.7.9" osisID="Rev.7.9!note.199">Literally, “he/it will not be able to count.”</note>could number, from all the nations standing before the throne and before the Lamb, wearing white garments, with branches in their hands.<verse eID="Rev.7.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.10" osisID="Rev.7.10" n="10"/>They cried out with a great voice, saying, “Salvation belongs to Yehovah, and to the Lamb!”<verse eID="Rev.7.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.11" osisID="Rev.7.11" n="11"/>And all standing before the throne, the messengers, the elders, and the living creatures, fallen on their faces before the throne, praying to Yehovah,<verse eID="Rev.7.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.12" osisID="Rev.7.12" n="12"/>saying, “Praise and thanksgiving, adoration, honor, glory, power, and strength be to our Elohim forever to ever. Amen!”<verse eID="Rev.7.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.13" osisID="Rev.7.13" n="13"/>Then one of the elders answered and said to me, “Who are these dressed in white garments? From what place do they come?”<verse eID="Rev.7.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.14" osisID="Rev.7.14" n="14"/>I said to Him, “Master, You know.” And He said to me, “These are those coming out of the midst of much sorrow. They have washed and purified their garments by the blood of the Lamb.”<verse eID="Rev.7.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.15" osisID="Rev.7.15" n="15"/>For this reason, they are before the throne of Yehovah and shall serve Him day and night. And this One sitting upon the throne will dwell among them.<verse eID="Rev.7.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.16" osisID="Rev.7.16" n="16"/>She <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="204" osisRef="Rev.7.16" osisID="Rev.7.16!note.204">Isaiah 39:10 has been translated to be, "They will not hunger nor thirst.”</note>will not hunger, nor thirst, nor be afflicted by the scorching heat and sun!<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="205" osisRef="Rev.7.16" osisID="Rev.7.16!note.205">Isiah 49:10.</note><verse eID="Rev.7.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.7.17" osisID="Rev.7.17" n="17"/>For the Lamb shall lead them to a well of waters, and Yehovah Elohim will wipe the tear from all faces.<verse eID="Rev.7.17"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.7"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.8" osisID="Rev.8" n="8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.1" osisID="Rev.8.1" n="1"/>And when he opened the seventh, all were silent in the heavens for a while.<verse eID="Rev.8.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.2" osisID="Rev.8.2" n="2"/>I saw seven messengers coming before Yehovah and they were given seven shofars.<verse eID="Rev.8.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.3" osisID="Rev.8.3" n="3"/>Another messenger came walking near to the holy place and in his hand was a censer of gold. He was given much incense to deliver prayers for the holy ones before the holy place and before the throne.<verse eID="Rev.8.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.4" osisID="Rev.8.4" n="4"/>The smoke from the incense of the holy ones went up from the hand of the messenger before Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.8.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.5" osisID="Rev.8.5" n="5"/>The messenger took the censer and filled it with fire and poured it out upon the earth and then came the sounds of thunders and lightnings!<verse eID="Rev.8.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.6" osisID="Rev.8.6" n="6"/>Then the seven messengers with the seven shofars prepared to blow.<verse eID="Rev.8.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.7" osisID="Rev.8.7" n="7"/>The first messenger blew, and there was hail and fire mixed with blood. Then it fell upon the earth, and a third of the trees burned, and all the greenery of the field burned.<verse eID="Rev.8.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.8" osisID="Rev.8.8" n="8"/>The second blew, and something like a great mountain, burned with fire, then went into the sea, and a third of the sea was blood.<verse eID="Rev.8.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.9" osisID="Rev.8.9" n="9"/>And a third of the creatures of the sea were put to death, and a third of the ships were sunk.<verse eID="Rev.8.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.10" osisID="Rev.8.10" n="10"/>Then the third blew, and a star burned and fell from the heavens upon a third of the springs of water,<verse eID="Rev.8.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.11" osisID="Rev.8.11" n="11"/>and the name of the star was Scarlet. A third of the waters became bitter. And because of its bitterness, many of the children of man died.<verse eID="Rev.8.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.12" osisID="Rev.8.12" n="12"/>The fourth blew, and a third part of the sun, moon, and the stars eclipsed, and a third of the day did not shine, nor the night.<verse eID="Rev.8.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.8.13" osisID="Rev.8.13" n="13"/>Then I saw and heard a messenger hovering in the heavens, and he shouted with a great voice, “Woe! Woe! Woe to those dwelling upon the earth, because the voice of the shofars - which are required - are yet to be blown from the three messengers!”<verse eID="Rev.8.13"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.8"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.9" osisID="Rev.9" n="9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.1" osisID="Rev.9.1" n="1"/>The fifth messenger blew, and I saw a star fall upon the earth from the heavens. And he was given the key to the deep.<verse eID="Rev.9.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.2" osisID="Rev.9.2" n="2"/>He opened the deep, and smoke went out from the deep like smoke from a large furnace, the sun and the air darkened from the smoke of the deep.<verse eID="Rev.9.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.3" osisID="Rev.9.3" n="3"/>From the smoke came frogs <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="213" osisRef="Rev.9.3" osisID="Rev.9.3!note.213">The word, צפרדעים (tzfarde'im) is explained in Revelation 16:13 as demons that look like frogs. If the marginal note is correct, then the translation should be, “From the smoke came swelling locusts.” But without a noun the sentence is incomplete.</note>upon the earth, and they were given authority to annihilate to destroy.<verse eID="Rev.9.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.4" osisID="Rev.9.4" n="4"/>They were told, “Do not damage the plants or the green grass of the field, nor any tree. Injure only the children of mankind who do not have the seal of Yehovah in their foreheads.”<verse eID="Rev.9.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.5" osisID="Rev.9.5" n="5"/>They (the frogs/locusts) were not given authority to kill them, but only to grieve them for five months. And their grief was like the pain when scorpions bite.<verse eID="Rev.9.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.6" osisID="Rev.9.6" n="6"/>In those days, you would be wiser to die, but not find it!<verse eID="Rev.9.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.7" osisID="Rev.9.7" n="7"/>The locusts <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="220" osisRef="Rev.9.7" osisID="Rev.9.7!note.220">This word can also mean,” those who are liars-in-wait” or “ambushers.”</note>are like the horses poised for battle. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="221" osisRef="Rev.9.7" osisID="Rev.9.7!note.221">Interestingly, Joel 2:25 references the children of Israel being restored what the locusts have eaten and also identifies the locusts as a great army of locusts: “Then I shall repay you the years that the swarming locust has eaten, the crawling locust, and the consuming locust, and the gnawing locust, My great army which I sent among you.” Also see Joel 1:4.</note>Upon their heads were crowns as of gold, their appearance like the appearance of mankind.<verse eID="Rev.9.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.8" osisID="Rev.9.8" n="8"/>Their hair like the hair of women, and their teeth, like the teeth of a lion,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="227" osisRef="Rev.9.8" osisID="Rev.9.8!note.227">This verse is like Joel 1:6, "With teeth like the teeth of a lion" שניו שני אריה .</note><verse eID="Rev.9.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.9" osisID="Rev.9.9" n="9"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" osisRef="Rev.9.9" osisID="Rev.9.9!note.absent">This verse does not exist in the Cochin Oo.1.16.2 manuscript</note><verse eID="Rev.9.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.10" osisID="Rev.9.10" n="10" subType="x-alt-9.9"/>and their rears were like the tails of a swarm. They were given authority to grieve the children of mankind for five months.<verse eID="Rev.9.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.11" osisID="Rev.9.11" n="11" subType="x-alt-9.10"/>Their king was a messenger from the deep. His name in the Hebrew language, Abaddon, and in the Greek language, Apollyon.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="234" osisRef="Rev.9.11" osisID="Rev.9.11!note.234">John, having been in the regions of modern-day Turkey and Greece, would have known some Greek. Josephus did the same kind of writing style. See The War of the Jews 5:4:2 - "This new-built part of the city was called "Bezetha," in our language, which, if interpreted in the Grecian language, may be called "the New City." – William Whiston, translator. However, the added vowel markers (nikkud) would indicate this was added later.</note><verse eID="Rev.9.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.12" osisID="Rev.9.12" n="12" subType="x-alt-9.11"/>Something like a cloud <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="236" osisRef="Rev.9.12" osisID="Rev.9.12!note.236">See Ezekiel 1:4 describing a wind coming from the north, a “great cloud” before Yehovah revealed Himself brilliantly like a rainbow and cloud (Ezekiel 1:27-28) before pronouncing judgment upon the children of Israel.</note>passed, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="237" osisRef="Rev.9.12" osisID="Rev.9.12!note.237">The Hebrew words used are “like/as dark storm clouds,” a simile for “more trouble is coming.” The use of a metaphor like "dark cloud" for "woe" shows the writer has familiarity with Hebrew.</note>and behold, the second is coming as clouds!<verse eID="Rev.9.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.13" osisID="Rev.9.13" n="13" subType="x-alt-9.12"/>And the sixth messenger blew. I heard a voice coming from the four corners of the holy place that is before Yehovah,<verse eID="Rev.9.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.14" osisID="Rev.9.14" n="14" subType="x-alt-9.13"/>saying to the sixth messenger who blew, “Go, and search for the four bound messengers in the great sea<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="241" osisRef="Rev.9.14" osisID="Rev.9.14!note.241">Presumed river Euphrates, but this is a transliteration from Greek. In the Tanakh it is Perat פְרָּׁת .</note> called, 'Euphrates.'”<verse eID="Rev.9.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.15" osisID="Rev.9.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-9.14"/>The four messengers were prepared to kill a third part <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="244" osisRef="Rev.9.15" osisID="Rev.9.15!note.244">See Ezekiel 5:12.</note>of the children of mankind in an hour, in a day, in a month, and in one year.<verse eID="Rev.9.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.16" osisID="Rev.9.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-9.15"/>And their riders were numbered a great many <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="245" osisRef="Rev.9.16" osisID="Rev.9.16!note.245">This word could also be translated as "myriad" or "ten thousand," but the context seems to indicate a great many (a thousand upon thousand). Therefore, it was translated as "great many" to indicate this large number. See Psalm 68:18.</note>thousands. And I heard their number.<verse eID="Rev.9.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.17" osisID="Rev.9.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-9.16"/>After this, I saw the horses in formation and those sitting upon them wearing garments of pitch, brimstone, and fire. The heads of the horses were like the heads of a lion, and from the horses' mouths went fire, smoke, and brimstone.<verse eID="Rev.9.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.18" osisID="Rev.9.18" n="18" subType="x-alt-9.17"/>And by these three (fire, smoke, brimstone) a third part of the children of mankind were killed,<verse eID="Rev.9.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.19" osisID="Rev.9.19" n="19" subType="x-alt-9.18"/>for their power was in their mouths, and their tails had heads like serpents, with which they kill.<verse eID="Rev.9.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.20" osisID="Rev.9.20" n="20" subType="x-alt-9.19"/>However, there were many children of mankind who will not be put to death by these plagues,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="251" osisRef="Rev.9.20" osisID="Rev.9.20!note.251">The word, הנגעים (ha' hagah'm) is from the נגע root which means “touch, reach, strike.” Also consider that leprosy was often associated with the evil tongue (lashon ha’ra’a). For Miriam spoke against Moses and was struck with leprosy (Numbers 12:1-15). And in Deuteronomy 24:9 it says to “remember what God did to Miriam” when she spoke against Moses (Deuteronomy 24:9). Thus, in the Cochin Hebrew Matthew 5:11it says, “Blessed are you when they insult you and persecute you saying against you לשון הרע (leshon ha’ra) (literally: “the evil tongue, language”) for the sake of my glory, to deceive.”</note> because they repented<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="252" osisRef="Rev.9.20" osisID="Rev.9.20!note.252">The word “repentance” comes from the noun teshuvah which means to repent and “return” to Yehovah.</note> of their evil works. They did not pray to Satan nor to idols of clay, stone, wood, silver, or gold - which are not able to walk or speak or hear.<verse eID="Rev.9.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.9.21" osisID="Rev.9.21" n="21" subType="x-alt-9.20"/>And there were those who did not repent for their fornications, nor for their thefts.<verse eID="Rev.9.21"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.9"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.10" osisID="Rev.10" n="10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.1" osisID="Rev.10.1" n="1"/>Then, I saw another messenger descending from the heavens, clothed in a cloud, with a rainbow over his head. His appearance was like the sun, his feet, like a flame of fire,<verse eID="Rev.10.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.2" osisID="Rev.10.2" n="2"/>and in his hand was an open scroll. He stepped with his right foot upon the sea and with his left upon the dry land.<verse eID="Rev.10.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.3" osisID="Rev.10.3" n="3"/>Then he cried with a great voice, like the cry of a lion. And within the hour that he shouted, the seven voices spoke with their voices.<verse eID="Rev.10.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.4" osisID="Rev.10.4" n="4"/>After they spoke with their voices, I wanted to write them. But I heard a small voice <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="262" osisRef="Rev.10.4" osisID="Rev.10.4!note.262">The phrase, בת קול (bat kol) is a rabbinic term that translates as, “Divine Voice” or “small voice of God,” dating from c. 100 BC through the Talmudic period of Jewish thought (Tractate Kallah Rabbati 8:2).</note>from the heavens say to me, "Shut them up and do not write them!"<verse eID="Rev.10.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.5" osisID="Rev.10.5" n="5"/>And the messenger which I saw standing on the sea and on the dry land lifted his right and left hands toward the heavens.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="265" osisRef="Rev.10.5" osisID="Rev.10.5!note.265">Daniel 12:7; ירם ימינו ושמאלו is an exact quote from the verse "One said to the man clothed in linen, who was above the water of the river, “How long until the end of these awful things? Then I heard the man dressed in linen, who was above the water of the river, swear by the Ever-Living One as he lifted his right hand and his left hand to heaven: “For a time, times, and half a time;-a and when the breaking of the power of the holy people comes to an end, then shall all these things be fulfilled.” – Jewish Publication Society (JPS), 1985 Translation.</note><verse eID="Rev.10.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.6" osisID="Rev.10.6" n="6"/>And he swore <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="266" osisRef="Rev.10.6" osisID="Rev.10.6!note.266">This verb can be either Nif’al yiqtol future, or Nif’al jussive future, “May he swear.”</note>by the Eternal Living One, who created the heavens and all that exists - the earth, the sea, and all that is in them – “There is no more time!"<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="267" osisRef="Rev.10.6" osisID="Rev.10.6!note.267">Compare to Daniel 12:6-7 Jewish Publication Society (JPS). 268Compare Amos 3:7 "Indeed, אדני יהוה (my Lord, Yehovah) does nothing – Without having revealed His purpose (mysteries) – To His servants the prophets." – Jewish Publication Society (JPS), 1985 Version.</note><verse eID="Rev.10.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.7" osisID="Rev.10.7" n="7"/>“Just as the seven messengers came, they blew the shofars, completing all the mysteries of Yehovah, announcing to His servants and to His prophets!"<verse eID="Rev.10.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.8" osisID="Rev.10.8" n="8"/>I heard again the small voice say to me, “Go and take the open scroll out of the hand of the messenger standing on the sea and on the dry land!”<verse eID="Rev.10.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.9" osisID="Rev.10.9" n="9"/>So, I went to the messenger and said, “Give me the scroll.” Then he said, “Take, and swallow, for it will be like honey in your mouth, but in your inward parts it will give you grief.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="275" osisRef="Rev.10.9" osisID="Rev.10.9!note.275">Compare to Ezekiel 3:3.</note><verse eID="Rev.10.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.10" osisID="Rev.10.10" n="10"/>I took the scroll from the hand of the messenger and then ate. It became like honey in my mouth. But after I swallowed, it was grief to my inward parts.<verse eID="Rev.10.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.10.11" osisID="Rev.10.11" n="11"/>Then he said to me, “You must prophesy again, to the nations, peoples, and to kings!”<verse eID="Rev.10.11"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.10"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.11" osisID="Rev.11" n="11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.1" osisID="Rev.11.1" n="1"/>Then he gave me a reed, like a rod, and he said, “Get up to measure the holy place of Yehovah and His altar, for there are intercessors in it.”<verse eID="Rev.11.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.2" osisID="Rev.11.2" n="2"/>Rather measure the inner holy place, but do not include <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="281" osisRef="Rev.11.2" osisID="Rev.11.2!note.281">The word זרוק (zerok), means to throw out - that is, to “not include”; remove something from private to public.</note>the outer court: for it is given to the nations to walk the holy city for forty-two months.”<verse eID="Rev.11.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.3" osisID="Rev.11.3" n="3"/>“For I give to my two witnesses to prophesy one thousand two hundred sixty days, dressed in sackcloth.”<verse eID="Rev.11.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.4" osisID="Rev.11.4" n="4"/>For these are the two olive trees and the two torches standing before Elohei (God) of the earth.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="283" osisRef="Rev.11.4" osisID="Rev.11.4!note.283">See also Zechariah 4:11-14 for the prophecy of the two messiahs (anointed ones).</note><verse eID="Rev.11.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.5" osisID="Rev.11.5" n="5"/>If anyone wants to harm these, then fire will come <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="284" osisRef="Rev.11.5" osisID="Rev.11.5!note.284">The yiqtol can be a simple future tense, the language of the Law, or an iterative action - past (Numbers 11:4-6) or future. See legend and definitions for more details.</note>and consume them.<verse eID="Rev.11.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.6" osisID="Rev.11.6" n="6"/>During the time they are prophets, they have the authority to shut the heavens so that no rain comes on the earth, and over the waters to turn them into blood,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="286" osisRef="Rev.11.6" osisID="Rev.11.6!note.286">See Exodus 7:20.</note> and to strike the earth with plagues, whenever they please.<verse eID="Rev.11.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.7" osisID="Rev.11.7" n="7"/>After their testimony was finished, He <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="287" osisRef="Rev.11.7" osisID="Rev.11.7!note.287">This is not a gender mismatch because this is a continued message from chapter 10. The “He” could be referring to Yehovah or Yeshua. The context is not clear.</note>brought forth the beast from the deep. The beast battled,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="288" osisRef="Rev.11.7" osisID="Rev.11.7!note.288">This word, מלחמה (milchama) is a noun but appears to be used as a verb as in Deuteronomy 2:9.</note> prevailed and killed them.<verse eID="Rev.11.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.8" osisID="Rev.11.8" n="8"/>Then their corpses were thrown <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="292" osisRef="Rev.11.8" osisID="Rev.11.8!note.292">This could be a tense error or could be a verb form used in a passive narrative voice as described in the footnote of Revelation 11:7.</note>into the streets of the Holy City, called Sodom and Egypt, and is also the exact place<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="293" osisRef="Rev.11.8" osisID="Rev.11.8!note.293">The scribe crossed out כיון and placed a small circle above to indicate a marginal note. The marginal note was included in the translation.</note> where our Master was crucified.<verse eID="Rev.11.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.9" osisID="Rev.11.9" n="9"/>And they of the nations and tribes, see their corpses for three and one-half days, but you <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="295" osisRef="Rev.11.9" osisID="Rev.11.9!note.295">The “you” is plural, and it is unclear to whom it is referring.</note>do not bury them. And they of the nations and tribes will see their corpses for three and one-half days, and they will not be buried.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="296" osisRef="Rev.11.9" osisID="Rev.11.9!note.296">Translation #2 would mean that the verb תקברו (tikberu) is a second person future, masculine plural. The structure of this verb still indicates that it will be “you (mp) that will not bury them. To correct this verb form, it would be the Nif’al verb נִקְבְרּו (nikberu) where the tav would be corrected to a nun. See interlinear table above for more details.</note><verse eID="Rev.11.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.10" osisID="Rev.11.10" n="10"/>Those dwelling upon the earth rejoiced over them and sent gifts to one another since the prophets brought a lot of grief to the inhabitants of the earth.<verse eID="Rev.11.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.11" osisID="Rev.11.11" n="11"/>And after three and a half days, they rose and stomped to their feet, and great fear and terror fell upon those seeing them.<verse eID="Rev.11.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.12" osisID="Rev.11.12" n="12"/>And they heard a voice saying, “Rise up!” Then they ascended to the heavens in a cloud, and they saw their enemies. And they heard a voice saying, “Rise up!” Then they ascended to the heavens in a cloud, and their enemies saw them.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="301" osisRef="Rev.11.12" osisID="Rev.11.12!note.301">It is unclear whether the "they" refers to the witnesses or the enemies, so both translations are included.</note><verse eID="Rev.11.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.13" osisID="Rev.11.13" n="13"/>At that time, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="302" osisRef="Rev.11.13" osisID="Rev.11.13!note.302">Can also mean, “And in the hour.”</note>there were tremblings of the earth; a tenth part of the city fell, and seven thousand died among the children of mankind. Others were terrified but we gave praise to Elohei (God) of the heavens.<verse eID="Rev.11.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.14" osisID="Rev.11.14" n="14"/>See! The second sorrow has come, and the third sorrow comes!<verse eID="Rev.11.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.15" osisID="Rev.11.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-12.1"/>Then the seventh messenger blew and there were many voices in heaven declaring the songs of creation.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="304" osisRef="Rev.11.15" osisID="Rev.11.15!note.304">Can also mean, “world.”</note> All things are subject to our Lord Yeshua, and he shall reign forever and ever.<verse eID="Rev.11.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.16" osisID="Rev.11.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-12.2"/>And the twenty-four elders sitting on their thrones before Yehovah fell upon their faces, praying to Yehovah,<verse eID="Rev.11.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.17" osisID="Rev.11.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-12.3"/>saying, “We praise You, Lord of Armies - who was, and is, and will be!”<verse eID="Rev.11.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.18" osisID="Rev.11.18" n="18" subType="x-alt-12.4"/>“The time of the nations <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="308" osisRef="Rev.11.18" osisID="Rev.11.18!note.308">See Genesis 15:16, Revelation 11:17 and Luke 21:24.</note>were filled. Therefore, the time of Your wrath and judgment of the dead comes: to give recompense to your servants, the holy prophets, and the righteous who revere You - for the small and the great; and to destroy those who corrupted the earth.” The Scripture: “And the nations were enraged, and Your wrath has come, and the time of the dead to be judged, and to give the reward to Your servants the prophets and to the set-apart ones, and to those who fear Your Name, small and great, and to destroy those who destroy the earth.”<verse eID="Rev.11.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.11.19" osisID="Rev.11.19" n="19" subType="x-alt-12.5"/>In the heavens, Yehovah's house of prayer <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="315" osisRef="Rev.11.19" osisID="Rev.11.19!note.315">See Isaiah 56:7, Ezekiel 43:10. The Temple is referred to as a "house of prayer."</note>opened and revealed the Ark of the tablets of the Covenant in His house. Then there are voices and thunders and lightnings<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="316" osisRef="Rev.11.19" osisID="Rev.11.19!note.316">See Exodus 20:18.</note> and tremors of the earth and great hail.<verse eID="Rev.11.19"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.11"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.12" osisID="Rev.12" n="12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.1" osisID="Rev.12.1" n="1" subType="x-alt-12.6"/>A great thing is seen again in the heavens: a woman clothed with the sun, the moon beneath her feet, and on her head a crown of twelve stars.<verse eID="Rev.12.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.2" osisID="Rev.12.2" n="2" subType="x-alt-12.7"/>She was with child and crying out, sat upon the birthing seat, and was in great pain because of her labor.<verse eID="Rev.12.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.3" osisID="Rev.12.3" n="3" subType="x-alt-12.8"/>Then there was another wonder in the heavens: I saw a dragon, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="325" osisRef="Rev.12.3" osisID="Rev.12.3!note.325">The first use of this word is in Genesis 1:21 where Jewish Publication Society (JPS) translates it as “sea monster.” Revelation 12:15 speaks of a "waters like a great sea from its mouth" so it appears to be associated with the sea.</note>and he had seven heads and ten horns, and on his heads were seven crowns.<verse eID="Rev.12.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.4" osisID="Rev.12.4" n="4" subType="x-alt-12.9"/>His tail took a third of the stars and threw them to the earth. Then the dragon stepped in front of the woman giving birth, in order to consume the male child being born of the mother.<verse eID="Rev.12.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.5" osisID="Rev.12.5" n="5" subType="x-alt-12.10"/>She gave birth to a son who leads the nations with a rod of iron. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="326" osisRef="Rev.12.5" osisID="Rev.12.5!note.326">See Psalm 2:9. This phrase is also used in Revelation 2:27A, and 19:15.</note>And he is taken before Yehovah and His throne.<verse eID="Rev.12.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.6" osisID="Rev.12.6" n="6" subType="x-alt-12.11"/>Because it was prepared for her by Yehovah, the woman walked the wilderness, in the place there supporting her, one thousand two hundred sixty days.<verse eID="Rev.12.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.7" osisID="Rev.12.7" n="7" subType="x-alt-12.12"/>Then war was made in the heavens. The messenger Michael and his messengers fought the dragon and his messengers; they fought with them.<verse eID="Rev.12.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.8" osisID="Rev.12.8" n="8" subType="x-alt-12.13"/>They did not prevail, so their place was not found in heaven any longer.<verse eID="Rev.12.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.9" osisID="Rev.12.9" n="9" subType="x-alt-12.14"/>And the great dragon - called Satan the Destroyer, the inciter of the entire world - was cast out. Him with his messengers were thrown to the earth, to dwell there.<verse eID="Rev.12.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.10" osisID="Rev.12.10" n="10" subType="x-alt-12.15a"/>I heard a great voice in the heavens that said, “Now we will see again the mighty works of our Elohim and His Messiah, because he (Satan) was their adversary the entire time.” I heard a great voice in heaven that said, "Now the mighty works were seen of our Elohim and His Messiah, because having been the adversary from all time, they are cast out."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="333" osisRef="Rev.12.10" osisID="Rev.12.10!note.333">It is uncertain if this is the Hit’pael or the Nit’pael verb form. Therefore, both translations were provided.</note><verse eID="Rev.12.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.11" osisID="Rev.12.11" n="11" subType="x-alt-12.15b"/>They prevailed over him by the blood of the Lamb and by the word of their testimonies, and they count their lives as nothing.<verse eID="Rev.12.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.12" osisID="Rev.12.12" n="12" subType="x-alt-12.16"/>So, rejoice, heavens and everything in it, but woe to those dwelling on land and sea! Satan is coming down to you with the fierceness of great anger, for he knows his time is shortened!<verse eID="Rev.12.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.13" osisID="Rev.12.13" n="13" subType="x-alt-12.17"/>Because the dragon saw that he was thrown to the earth, pursued the woman who gave birth.<verse eID="Rev.12.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.14" osisID="Rev.12.14" n="14" subType="x-alt-12.18a"/>Then the woman is given two wings like a great eagle. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="346" osisRef="Rev.12.14" osisID="Rev.12.14!note.346">Compare to Exodus 19:4, Isaiah 43:19 and chapter 41.</note>She walks to the wilderness in a place belonging to Yehovah supporting her there, away from the dragon, for a season and two and one-half seasons.<verse eID="Rev.12.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.15" osisID="Rev.12.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-12.18b"/>From his mouth the dragon cast waters behind her like a great sea, so to silence her.<verse eID="Rev.12.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.16" osisID="Rev.12.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-12.19"/>But because he opened his mouth and swallowed the waters the dragon cast behind her, the earth delivered the woman.<verse eID="Rev.12.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.12.17" osisID="Rev.12.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-12.20"/>The dragon, filled with anger for the woman, went to war with the remnant of her seed - those doing the commands of Yehovah and believing in the testimony of His Messiah.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="351" osisRef="Rev.12.17" osisID="Rev.12.17!note.351">See Matthew 5:17-20.</note><verse eID="Rev.12.17"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.12"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.13" osisID="Rev.13" n="13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.1a" osisID="Rev.13.1a" n="1a" subType="x-alt-13.1"/>Then I stepped <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="355" osisRef="Rev.13.1a" osisID="Rev.13.1a!note.355">This can also be “marched” indicating an anticipation of military stance.</note>onto the sand of the sea.<verse eID="Rev.13.1a"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.1b" osisID="Rev.13.1b" n="1b" subType="x-alt-13.2"/>And I saw a beast <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="356" osisRef="Rev.13.1b" osisID="Rev.13.1b!note.356">This word is feminine. So, every verb or adjective that refers to this noun, should be feminine. However, it is masculine all the way through. It is possible that חיה was considered a masculine word during the second Temple time, and that would mean that there are no gender mismatches with the verb forms. Could the grammatical gender confusion hint to an end of day event of “gender confusion?”</note>come out of the sea, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="357" osisRef="Rev.13.1b" osisID="Rev.13.1b!note.357">We see the dragon (i.e. the sea monster) and the first beast coming out of the sea, both have 7 heads and 10 horns, which are the leaders (governments) of the nation’s coming together to overcome the earth. For it becomes clear, the dragon and the beast are the same (i.e. Satan) (Revelation 12:3 &amp; 13:2). And isn’t it appropriate that the sea monster comes out of the sea as described in this verse?</note>and it had seven heads and ten horns, and on its horns ten crowns, and on its head’s, names of slander and mockery.<verse eID="Rev.13.1b"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.2" osisID="Rev.13.2" n="2" subType="x-alt-13.3"/>The beast that I saw was like a horse; its legs and feet like the legs and feet of bears, and its mouth like the mouth of a lion. And the dragon<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="359" osisRef="Rev.13.2" osisID="Rev.13.2!note.359">The first use of this word is in Genesis 1:21 where Jewish Publication Society (JPS) translates it as “sea monster.” Revelation 12:15 speaks of a "waters like a great sea from its mouth" so there is some association with the sea.</note> gave it (the beast) his power and his throne, military strength<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="360" osisRef="Rev.13.2" osisID="Rev.13.2!note.360">Contrast with Revelation 12:10. See Genesius' explanation of this word גבורה comparing Judges 8:21 and Isaiah 36:5.</note> and great courage.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="361" osisRef="Rev.13.2" osisID="Rev.13.2!note.361">The adjective and the noun must match in gender, number, and definiteness. When the adjective does not match the noun, it is called a “predicate adjective.” A predicate adjective can be placed anywhere within the sentence. The sentence then becomes a nominal sentence and is translated as, “Courage is great.” However, it appears John is using this as an attributive adjective, and I have chosen to translate it as “great courage.” There are many similar instances of this anomaly within this manuscript.</note><verse eID="Rev.13.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.3" osisID="Rev.13.3" n="3" subType="x-alt-13.4"/>I saw one of its heads resembling a wound leading to death, but the wound was healed. So, the whole world was astonished by the beast.<verse eID="Rev.13.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.4" osisID="Rev.13.4" n="4" subType="x-alt-13.5"/>Those praying to the dragon gave power and dominion to the beast. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="365" osisRef="Rev.13.4" osisID="Rev.13.4!note.365">Revelation 13 begins with John taking a strong military stance on the sands of the sea. He is prepared for the battle that is about to begin. Then suddenly the beast (i.e. Satan) comes up out of the sea and is not in the likeness of a leopard, but in the likeness of a horse with legs and feet of a bear, and the mouth of a lion. Then the beast is allowed to battle with the people of Yehovah but prevail over all the people of the earth (Revelation 13:7). And everyone who is not marked for Yehovah, are praying to the dragon which gives power to the beast. See Revelation 4:10 about praying (Hit’pael) and may possibly be dialoguing with the beast that ultimately gives it power.</note>And those praying said, “Who is like this beast and who is able to battle<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="366" osisRef="Rev.13.4" osisID="Rev.13.4!note.366">The word, ללחום (l’lchom) can mean “to eat” or “to battle.” The first use of it for “to battle” is The Book of Maccabees 9:30</note> with it?”<verse eID="Rev.13.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.5" osisID="Rev.13.5" n="5" subType="x-alt-13.6"/>The beast was permitted to open its mouth to speak words of wonders and cursing until imprisoned for the time of forty-two months.<verse eID="Rev.13.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.6" osisID="Rev.13.6" n="6" subType="x-alt-13.7"/>It opened its mouth to curse against Yehovah, and against those living in the heavens.<verse eID="Rev.13.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.7" osisID="Rev.13.7" n="7" subType="x-alt-13.8"/>It was permitted to battle with the holy people but prevail over all the peoples and nations.<verse eID="Rev.13.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.8" osisID="Rev.13.8" n="8" subType="x-alt-13.9"/>All those living on the earth prayed to it - all whose names not written in the Scroll of Life belonging to the slain Lamb, who was from the beginning of the creation of the world.<verse eID="Rev.13.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.9" osisID="Rev.13.9" n="9" subType="x-alt-13.10"/>He who has ears, hear!<verse eID="Rev.13.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.10" osisID="Rev.13.10" n="10" subType="x-alt-13.11"/>If one drives another into captivity, he will go into captivity, and if one kills a soul by the sword, he will be killed by the sword.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="374" osisRef="Rev.13.10" osisID="Rev.13.10!note.374">Jeremiah 15:2.</note> But with the holy ones there is hope and faith!<verse eID="Rev.13.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.11" osisID="Rev.13.11" n="11" subType="x-alt-13.12"/>Then I saw another beast who came out of the ground. It had two horns like a lamb, and it was speaking like the dragon.<verse eID="Rev.13.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.12" osisID="Rev.13.12" n="12" subType="x-alt-13.13"/>It did great wonders before the first beast. And it made those dwelling on the ground pray <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="375" osisRef="Rev.13.12" osisID="Rev.13.12!note.375">See Revelation 4:10 for first use of this word, “pray” in Hit’pael verb binyan stem.</note>to the first beast, whose wounds were healed.<verse eID="Rev.13.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.13" osisID="Rev.13.13" n="13" subType="x-alt-13.14"/>It made fire fall from the heavens before mankind.<verse eID="Rev.13.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.14" osisID="Rev.13.14" n="14" subType="x-alt-13.15"/>It inflamed the children of mankind with signs and wonders. And it told those dwelling on the earth to make an idol<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="377" osisRef="Rev.13.14" osisID="Rev.13.14!note.377">The Hebrew word for “idol” is פסל (pesel) and means that it was designed to be worshipped. And in Revelation 13:15, the pesel (idol) of the beast receives a spirit and soul and is able to speak. Therefore, if this is created by man, then this appears to hint of something similar to a hologram, or tangible hologram that could potentially manifest in every home.</note> of the beast, whose wounds from the sword were healed.<verse eID="Rev.13.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.15" osisID="Rev.13.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-13.16"/>And it was permitted to give to the idol <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="379" osisRef="Rev.13.15" osisID="Rev.13.15!note.379">It is important to note that all sculptures were not created as pesels (idols). But if a sculpture is worshipped or prayed to, then it becomes a pesel (i.e. idol). Consider in Exodus 20 when the children of Israel complained about the manna, calling it “worthless,” Yehovah sent fiery serpents to bite the children of Israel, and many died. When they repented, Moses prayed and Yehovah instructed Moses to make a bronze serpent on a pole so that when the children of Israel looked upon that bronze serpent, they would be healed. However, during the days of King Hezekiah of Judah, the children of Israel lost sight of Yehovah, burning incense (i.e., “making prayers,” Revelation 8:4-5) to that serpent. And, Hezekiah, a righteous man, destroyed the serpent on the pole (for no one should pray to any object: Exodus 20:4-5, 2 Kings 18:1-4). Later, Yeshua said that He would be “raised up like the serpent on the pole,” in John 3:14. It is also interesting to note, that according to Larry Palevsky, MD and Sherri Tenpenny, MD’s video regarding Dr. Bryan Ardis snake venom theory discusses the possibility of snake venom being added to the Covid-19 vaccine. Video date: June 10, 2022. Video website: https://t.me/covid_vaccine_injuries/2032. It is also interesting to note that the word כֶׁשֶף (kesef) in Numbers 21 and in Isiah 47:12 means, “sorceries,” and the Greek equivalent word is φαρμακείᾳ (pharmakeia). This seems to imply that pharmaceutical companies (having the logo of the serpent on the pole) seem to be a key player in the killing of the people (with their witchcraft) through pharmaceuticals when they pray to the pharmaceutical companies. See Revelation 18:23 for more details when the great city Babel is destroyed and her “witchcrafts” (i.e. pharmaceuticals) are no more.</note>of the beast a spirit and a soul, so that it would be able to speak. And whoever did not want to pray<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="380" osisRef="Rev.13.15" osisID="Rev.13.15!note.380">See Revelation 4:1 for the first use of this word. This word was crossed out in the Cochin Oo.1.16.2. appears to be an error and was corrected in the margin.</note> to the idol of the beast would be killed.<verse eID="Rev.13.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.16" osisID="Rev.13.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-13.17"/>It made them all - the great and the small, the wealthy and the poor, the slaves and the free – take a sign<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="382" osisRef="Rev.13.16" osisID="Rev.13.16!note.382">The first use of this word סימן (siman) is translated as “omen” in the Mishnah, Berakhot (“Blessings”) dating from c. 190 CE to 230 CE. This word is derived from the Greek word sīmā, “sign,” “mark,” or “omen.” The Aramaic word is סִימָּׁנָּׁא (sīmānā) and in Arabic امَيسِ (sīmā).</note>, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="383" osisRef="Rev.13.16" osisID="Rev.13.16!note.383">This was translated as "sign" to depict the difference between it and the tav ( תב ) mark mentioned in Revelation 7:3. The first publication found with this word is in the Mishnah, Berakhot published c.190 to c230 CE and was translated as “omen.”</note>in his right hand or in their foreheads,<verse eID="Rev.13.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.17" osisID="Rev.13.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-13.18"/>so that the only one who has the sign in his hands, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="387" osisRef="Rev.13.17" osisID="Rev.13.17!note.387">The word “hand” is singular in the previous verse is plural in this verse while the word “foreheads” is plural in the previous verse but is singular in this verse.</note>or forehead, or the name belonging to the beast, or the number of its name would be able to buy or sell.<verse eID="Rev.13.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.13.18" osisID="Rev.13.18" n="18" subType="x-alt-13.19"/>Here is wisdom and understanding: Appoint one who understands, to calculate the number of the beast, for it is a number belonging to a person, for you will discover the sum is six hundred and sixty-six.<verse eID="Rev.13.18"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.13"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.14" osisID="Rev.14" n="14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.1" osisID="Rev.14.1" n="1"/>Then I saw a Lamb standing on Mount Zion, and with him one hundred and forty-four thousand,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="388" osisRef="Rev.14.1" osisID="Rev.14.1!note.388">This is not “Jews only.” See Revelation 7:4 for more details on the Tribes of Israel.</note> on whose foreheads were inscribed the Name of His Father.<verse eID="Rev.14.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.2" osisID="Rev.14.2" n="2"/>I heard a voice <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="391" osisRef="Rev.14.2" osisID="Rev.14.2!note.391">Literally, "one voice."</note>from the heavens, like a tumult of many waters, and as the sound of many voices. The voice that I heard was like the voice of a harp.<verse eID="Rev.14.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.3" osisID="Rev.14.3" n="3"/>They were singing <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="392" osisRef="Rev.14.3" osisID="Rev.14.3!note.392">The (root: זמר ) means “song, might, and plucking”: Hebrew and Aramaic Lexicon of the Old Testament (HALOT). According to Cohanim (Priests) in Israel, this word means the “Power of God in the music.” See Revelation 5:9 for more details.</note>the renewed song before the throne, the four living creatures, and the elders. No one was able to learn this song but the hundred and forty-four thousand that He redeemed from the ground.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="393" osisRef="Rev.14.3" osisID="Rev.14.3!note.393">It appears the 144,000 are both men, women, and possibly children from the past and present as some will be raised from the dead (i.e. the ground) and those living will be changed at a “twinkling of an eye” as in 1 Corinthians 15:52. Also see footnote for Revelation 7:3 for more details.</note><verse eID="Rev.14.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.4" osisID="Rev.14.4" n="4"/>These are those <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="397" osisRef="Rev.14.4" osisID="Rev.14.4!note.397">This word can be both men and women (i.e. common pronoun). Third common plural or masculine plural pronoun. In other words, this can be both men, women, and possibly children chosen to be one of the 144,000.</note>who did not sin with women <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="398" osisRef="Rev.14.4" osisID="Rev.14.4!note.398">Can mean “idolatry,” as in Isaiah 1:21; Jeremiah 2:2; 3:7-11; Ezekiel 16; Nahum 3:1-4; Hosea 1:3, 4:15; 5:5; Lamentations 1:1 and Matthew 12:39.</note>and are like virgins <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="399" osisRef="Rev.14.4" osisID="Rev.14.4!note.399">The kaf (כ) prefix means, “like” or “as.” This means that the 144,000 are not virgins, but they are “like virgins.”</note>who go after the Lamb and were redeemed for the firstborn of Yehovah and the Lamb.<verse eID="Rev.14.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.5" osisID="Rev.14.5" n="5"/>In their spirit is no deceit, for they are blameless before the throne of Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.14.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.6" osisID="Rev.14.6" n="6"/>Then I saw a messenger hovering in the midst of the heavens, and he had a powerful notice <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="401" osisRef="Rev.14.6" osisID="Rev.14.6!note.401">This idiom also means, “powerful writing.”</note>to announce to the inhabitants on the earth, the peoples and the nations.<verse eID="Rev.14.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.7" osisID="Rev.14.7" n="7"/>He said in a loud voice, “Fear Yehovah! Pray to Him for the day of judgment comes. Pray to Him who created the heavens, the earth, the sea, and the wellsprings.<verse eID="Rev.14.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.8" osisID="Rev.14.8" n="8"/>After this, the voice of another messenger shouted, “Fallen! Fallen is the great city Babel!<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="405" osisRef="Rev.14.8" osisID="Rev.14.8!note.405">Isaiah 21:9.</note> Because she<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="406" osisRef="Rev.14.8" osisID="Rev.14.8!note.406">Although the verb is masculine singular, the word it is associated with is feminine, so this is a gender mismatch and the pronoun "he/it" was used instead of “she/it.”</note> was appeased with the wine of her fornication for all nations.”<verse eID="Rev.14.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.9" osisID="Rev.14.9" n="9"/>Then the third messenger came and said in a loud voice, “If anyone prays to the idol of the beast and takes the sign on his hand or on his forehead,”<verse eID="Rev.14.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.10" osisID="Rev.14.10" n="10"/>“then he will drink from the wine of Yehovah’s wrath. There is the cup of wrath. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="409" osisRef="Rev.14.10" osisID="Rev.14.10!note.409">See Jeremiah 25:15-17.</note>And he will be punished with fire and brimstone in the presence of His holy messengers and the Lamb.”<verse eID="Rev.14.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.11" osisID="Rev.14.11" n="11"/>So, the smoke from their punishment will ascend forever. There will be no rest day or night for those who took the sign and prayed to the idol.<verse eID="Rev.14.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.12" osisID="Rev.14.12" n="12"/>In this there is hope for the holy ones who do the commandments of Yehovah and believe in Yeshua.<verse eID="Rev.14.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.13" osisID="Rev.14.13" n="13"/>Then I heard a small voice from heaven saying to me, “Write! 'From this time forward, the holy ones, they - the dead due to the sanctification of the Name<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="411" osisRef="Rev.14.13" osisID="Rev.14.13!note.411">Found first in Sefer HaKana 2:1 about c. 100 AD.</note> - (and truly the Spirit said) they live by their works, because their works follow them.'”<verse eID="Rev.14.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.14" osisID="Rev.14.14" n="14"/>I saw one sitting on a white cloud who had the appearance of a man, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="416" osisRef="Rev.14.14" osisID="Rev.14.14!note.416">See Ezekiel 1:5.</note>and on his head was a crown of gold, and in his hand was a sickle.<verse eID="Rev.14.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.15" osisID="Rev.14.15" n="15"/>And another messenger came from the house of prayer <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="417" osisRef="Rev.14.15" osisID="Rev.14.15!note.417">See Revelation 11:19.</note>and cried in a loud voice to the one who sat on the cloud, “Harvest with your sickle! For the time of harvest has come!”<verse eID="Rev.14.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.16" osisID="Rev.14.16" n="16"/>So, he who sat on the cloud struck with his sickle and he harvested.<verse eID="Rev.14.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.17" osisID="Rev.14.17" n="17"/>Another messenger went to the heavens, and in his hand was a snare.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="419" osisRef="Rev.14.17" osisID="Rev.14.17!note.419">The word מעגל (ma’agal) literally means, “a circle” but is used figuratively as a snare since a snare is a circle of rope or line. See Brown Driver Briggs (BDB) definition.</note><verse eID="Rev.14.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.18" osisID="Rev.14.18" n="18"/>Another messenger came out from the altar having authority over fire and cried out with a loud voice to the one who had the sickle, saying, "Strike with your sickle and reap the grapes of the land, for its fruit are ripe."<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="421" osisRef="Rev.14.18" osisID="Rev.14.18!note.421">There is a sof passuq indicating the end of a sentence or phrase.</note> And the messenger harvested with his sickle the grapes of the land and threw them into the great winepress of the wrath of Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.14.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.14.19" osisID="Rev.14.19" n="19-20" subType="x-alt-14.19"/>And the winepress was outside the city, and the blood of the winepress rose to the bridles of the horses through one thousand six hundred field graves. And the winepress was outside the city, and the blood of the winepress rose to the bridles of the horses trampling one thousand six hundred field graves.<verse eID="Rev.14.19"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.14"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.15" osisID="Rev.15" n="15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.1" osisID="Rev.15.1" n="1"/>Then I saw miracles and great wonders in the heavens: seven messengers came, and they had the seven last plagues. In them is the total wrath of the anger of Yehovah!<verse eID="Rev.15.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.2" osisID="Rev.15.2" n="2"/>I saw something like a sea of glass <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="428" osisRef="Rev.15.2" osisID="Rev.15.2!note.428">If a saucer or bowl צלוחית (tzlochit) is made of glass, a sea of saucer or sea bowl could be translated “a very large sea of glass.” צלוחית also has the meaning of a flask with a large belly at the bottom.</note>mixed with fire. And which those who overcame the beast, and its idol were standing by the sea of glass, and they had lyres of Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.15.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.3" osisID="Rev.15.3" n="3"/>They sang <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="433" osisRef="Rev.15.3" osisID="Rev.15.3!note.433">The (root: זמר ) means song, might and plucking: Hebrew and Aramaic Lexicon of the Old Testament (HALOT). According to some kohanim (priests) in Israel, this word means the “Power of God in the music.” See Revelation 5:9.</note>the song of Moses and the song of the Lamb, saying, “Great are the works of Yehovah, Elohim of Armies!<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="434" osisRef="Rev.15.3" osisID="Rev.15.3!note.434">The first occurrence is seen in Hosea 12:6.</note> Justice and truth are your ways, King of kings!”<verse eID="Rev.15.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.4" osisID="Rev.15.4" n="4"/>“Who does not fear You and give praise to Your Name? For You alone <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="439" osisRef="Rev.15.4" osisID="Rev.15.4!note.439">See Genesis 43:32 for translation of the word לבדו (levado), “by Himself.” Genesis 2:18 translates this as, "alone."</note>are holy! People shall come and bow down before You, for Your judgments are revealed!”<verse eID="Rev.15.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.5" osisID="Rev.15.5" n="5"/>And after this, I saw that the Ark of the Covenant in the heavens opened.<verse eID="Rev.15.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.6" osisID="Rev.15.6" n="6"/>Then the seven messengers having the seven plagues went out, clothed in white garments, belts of gold on their waist.<verse eID="Rev.15.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.7" osisID="Rev.15.7" n="7"/>And one of the four living creatures gave the seven messengers seven bowls of gold filled with the wrath of Yehovah, who lives everlasting to everlasting.<verse eID="Rev.15.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.15.8" osisID="Rev.15.8" n="8"/>Then the house of prayer was filled with smoke, and the glory of Yehovah filled the tabernacle. Thus no one was permitted inside until the seven messengers completed the seven plagues.<verse eID="Rev.15.8"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.15"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.16" osisID="Rev.16" n="16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.1" osisID="Rev.16.1" n="1"/>Then I heard a voice in the house of prayer saying to the seven messengers, “Go and pour out the bowls of the wrath of Yehovah upon the ground!”<verse eID="Rev.16.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.2" osisID="Rev.16.2" n="2"/>So, the first one poured out his bowl upon the ground and a skin disease <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="453" osisRef="Rev.16.2" osisID="Rev.16.2!note.453">This word could refer to several different skin conditions and diseases. The symptoms described in the Torah are unrecognized by physicians today. This is most likely a condition that once existed and will return in the final days.</note>broke out on anyone who had the sign of the beast and was praying to its idol.<verse eID="Rev.16.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.3" osisID="Rev.16.3" n="3"/>And the second messenger poured his bowl into the sea, and it became blood like from one dying. And everything in the sea died.<verse eID="Rev.16.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.4" osisID="Rev.16.4" n="4"/>Then the third poured his bowl into the wells of water, and it became blood.<verse eID="Rev.16.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.5" osisID="Rev.16.5" n="5"/>And I heard the messenger say, “Yehovah the Righteous, who was and is holy; You, who gave this judgment!”<verse eID="Rev.16.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.6" osisID="Rev.16.6" n="6"/>“Because they spilled the blood of the holy ones and the prophets like water. And you gave them blood to drink, for they deserve it.” “Because they spilled the blood of the holy ones and the prophets. And you gave them blood to drink like water, for they deserve it.”<verse eID="Rev.16.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.7" osisID="Rev.16.7" n="7"/>Then I heard another messenger inside the holy place say, “Yes, Elohim of Armies, your judgments are righteous!”<verse eID="Rev.16.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.8" osisID="Rev.16.8" n="8"/>Then the fourth poured his bowl into the sun, giving it authority to cause heat on mankind.<verse eID="Rev.16.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.9" osisID="Rev.16.9" n="9"/>So, it became very hot for mankind, and Yehovah cursed them with plagues, but they did not repent nor give Him honor.<verse eID="Rev.16.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.10" osisID="Rev.16.10" n="10"/>Then the fifth poured out his bowl on the throne of the beast and concealed it. So, they gnawed their tongues in the face of despair.<verse eID="Rev.16.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.11" osisID="Rev.16.11" n="11"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" osisRef="Rev.16.11" osisID="Rev.16.11!note.absent">This verse does not exist in the Cochin Oo.1.16.2 manuscript</note><verse eID="Rev.16.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.12" osisID="Rev.16.12" n="12" subType="x-alt-16.11"/>Then the sixth poured out his bowl on the Euphrates River. So, the river dried up and became a ruin for the kings<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="462" osisRef="Rev.16.12" osisID="Rev.16.12!note.462">In ancient times מלכים (melachim) meant rulers, or heads of tribes and over time it became kings.</note> of the East.<verse eID="Rev.16.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.13" osisID="Rev.16.13" n="13" subType="x-alt-16.12"/>Then I saw three unclean creeping things, like frogs, coming out of the mouths of the dragon, the beast, and the false prophet.<verse eID="Rev.16.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.14" osisID="Rev.16.14" n="14" subType="x-alt-16.13"/>For these are the same spirits of Satan doing wonders and signs, who are going to the kings of the earth, and to all the world to gather them for the battle on the day of the Almighty Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.16.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.15" osisID="Rev.16.15" n="15" subType="x-alt-16.14"/>See, I will come as a thief. <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="464" osisRef="Rev.16.15" osisID="Rev.16.15!note.464">See Revelation 3:3 for more details.</note>So, holy is he <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="465" osisRef="Rev.16.15" osisID="Rev.16.15!note.465">The Hebrew word אילו (eylu) is plural and should be translated as "these" or "those," but the rest of the sentence is singular, therefore it was modified in English for number consistency.</note>who does not sleep and keeps himself; so that he does not go naked without clothes so that he does not reveal his nakedness.<verse eID="Rev.16.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.16" osisID="Rev.16.16" n="16" subType="x-alt-16.15"/>And he gathered them in one place called in the Hebrew tongue Har Mageddan.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="467" osisRef="Rev.16.16" osisID="Rev.16.16!note.467">The scribe added nikkudot (vowel markings) to clarify the pronunciation, but was probably originally two words, "Har (mountain) Megeddan."</note><verse eID="Rev.16.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.17" osisID="Rev.16.17" n="17" subType="x-alt-16.16"/>Then the seventh poured out his bowl on the atmosphere, and a voice came from the throne and said, “This one has passed.”<verse eID="Rev.16.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.18" osisID="Rev.16.18" n="18" subType="x-alt-16.17"/>And there were voices, thunders, lightnings, and earthquakes like never before.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="469" osisRef="Rev.16.18" osisID="Rev.16.18!note.469">Literally, "not be of eternity."</note><verse eID="Rev.16.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.19" osisID="Rev.16.19" n="19" subType="x-alt-16.18"/>Thus, the great city became three parts, and the cities of the nations fell. So, the great city Babel was remembered before Yehovah who gave her the cup with wine, which is the wrath of His anger.<verse eID="Rev.16.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.20" osisID="Rev.16.20" n="20" subType="x-alt-16.19"/>Then all the mountains and hills fled and were seen no more.<verse eID="Rev.16.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.16.21" osisID="Rev.16.21" n="21" subType="x-alt-16.20"/>And great hail fell upon man from the heavens, and they cursed Yehovah because of the plague of the hail.<verse eID="Rev.16.21"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.16"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.17" osisID="Rev.17" n="17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.1" osisID="Rev.17.1" n="1"/>Then one of the seven messengers had one of the seven bowls came and spoke to me. And he said, “Come, and I will show you the judgment of the great harlot who sits upon many waters.”<verse eID="Rev.17.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.2" osisID="Rev.17.2" n="2"/>“The kings <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="471" osisRef="Rev.17.2" osisID="Rev.17.2!note.471">See Revelation 16:12. This word can mean “rulers, kings, or sovereigns.”</note>of the earth committed fornication with her and they became intoxicated by her wine.”<verse eID="Rev.17.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.3" osisID="Rev.17.3" n="3"/>Then he brought me into the wilderness. And I saw the woman sitting on the beast, whose appearance was like the appearance of lilies and was full of slanderous names, having seven heads and ten horns.<verse eID="Rev.17.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.4" osisID="Rev.17.4" n="4"/>And the woman <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="473" osisRef="Rev.17.4" osisID="Rev.17.4!note.473">Literally, "And the woman, he was clothed in…" This seems to hint of an end of day “gender confusion” problem.</note>was clothed in red garments with the appearance of lilies. And on her was a great exceeding gold with precious stones and pearls. A golden cup full of impurities was in her hand.<verse eID="Rev.17.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.5" osisID="Rev.17.5" n="5"/>And written on her forehead was the name of the mystery of the great city, Babel, the mother of harlots and the defiled of the earth.<verse eID="Rev.17.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.6" osisID="Rev.17.6" n="6"/>And I saw that the woman drank the blood of the holy ones and the blood of the congregations of Yeshua. I had great astonishment when I saw her.<verse eID="Rev.17.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.7" osisID="Rev.17.7" n="7"/>And the messenger said to me, “Why are you astonished? I want to tell you the mystery of the woman, and of the beast which she sat upon — he has seven heads and ten horns.”<verse eID="Rev.17.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.8" osisID="Rev.17.8" n="8"/>The beast that you saw was and was not, and it will come once more from the deep and will go to Sheol. The people of the earth will be amazed at it when they see the beast that was and is not even though it is.<verse eID="Rev.17.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.9" osisID="Rev.17.9" n="9"/>And here is the matter that requires intellect, wisdom and understanding: the seven heads are seven mountains that the woman<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="480" osisRef="Rev.17.9" osisID="Rev.17.9!note.480">The verb “dwelling” does not match the gender of the noun “woman.” The literal translation reads as follows, “He, the woman, dwells upon…” This again seems to hint to a “gender confusion” problem relating to the woman and the beast in the end of day events.</note> dwells upon; they are the seven kings.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="481" osisRef="Rev.17.9" osisID="Rev.17.9!note.481">"Kings” and “messengers" are spelled the same in Hebrew without the nikkudot (vowel markings), but "kings" fits the context of the verse. .</note><verse eID="Rev.17.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.10" osisID="Rev.17.10" n="10"/>But five of these fell, and one is, the second is not yet; but when <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="482" osisRef="Rev.17.10" osisID="Rev.17.10!note.482">This can be “if he returns” or “when he returns.”</note>he comes, it must be for a short time.<verse eID="Rev.17.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.11" osisID="Rev.17.11" n="11"/>And the beast that is not is the eighth for he was one of the seven but went to Sheol.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="485" osisRef="Rev.17.11" osisID="Rev.17.11!note.485">Verses ten and eleven seem to imply that one of the heads died (one of the seven that went to Sheol), and when it was resurrected it became the eighth head.</note><verse eID="Rev.17.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.12" osisID="Rev.17.12" n="12"/>And the ten horns that you saw are ten kings who have not yet received their kingdoms, but they will rule as kings with the beast for a short time.<verse eID="Rev.17.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.13" osisID="Rev.17.13" n="13"/>And they have one purpose, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="487" osisRef="Rev.17.13" osisID="Rev.17.13!note.487">The Biblical Hebrew word, מחשבה (machshavah) means, “thought, plan, or device.” Interestingly, a grammatical shift occurs in modern Hebrew to include a unified thought. Therefore, this word מחשבה (machshevah) translates as, “her/its computer” in modern Hebrew. It is important to note that the woman and the beast are both feminine nouns. This grammatical shift seems to hint of a modern day supercomputer.</note>and they will give their power and strength to the beast.<verse eID="Rev.17.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.14" osisID="Rev.17.14" n="14"/>And they will make war with the Lamb, and the Lamb will prevail over them. For He is Lord of lords, and King of kings;<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="488" osisRef="Rev.17.14" osisID="Rev.17.14!note.488">This can also be translated as “Ruler of rulers.”</note> and with Him are the chosen and the faithful.<verse eID="Rev.17.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.15" osisID="Rev.17.15" n="15"/>And he said to me, “The waters where the harlot lives that I saw are peoples, corporations, and nations.”<verse eID="Rev.17.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.16" osisID="Rev.17.16" n="16"/>And the ten horns that you saw on the beast will hate the harlot, and will make a wasteland of her, and eat her flesh and burn her with fire.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="492" osisRef="Rev.17.16" osisID="Rev.17.16!note.492">Literally, “you will burn her,” but that does not fit in the context.</note><verse eID="Rev.17.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.17" osisID="Rev.17.17" n="17"/>For Yehovah allowed their hearts to do His will, to give her riches to the beast until she is devastated by the words of Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.17.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.17.18" osisID="Rev.17.18" n="18"/>And the woman that you saw is the great city, the kings over all the kings of the earth.<verse eID="Rev.17.18"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.17"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.18" osisID="Rev.18" n="18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.1" osisID="Rev.18.1" n="1"/>Afterwards, I saw another messenger descending from the heavens, and he had power and great strength, and his light illuminated the earth.<verse eID="Rev.18.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.2" osisID="Rev.18.2" n="2"/>Then he cried with all his power, and said, “Fallen! Fallen! Is the great city Babel! <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="495" osisRef="Rev.18.2" osisID="Rev.18.2!note.495">See Isaiah 21:9.</note>For she was the dwelling place of the adversaries to hide all the unclean birds and creeping things.”<verse eID="Rev.18.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.3" osisID="Rev.18.3" n="3"/>“Because all of them drank of her wine, and the rulers of the earth committed fornication with her. And all the merchants became rich, for their lust was great.”<verse eID="Rev.18.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.4" osisID="Rev.18.4" n="4"/>Then I heard another voice from the heavens say, “Come out of her, my people, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="497" osisRef="Rev.18.4" osisID="Rev.18.4!note.497">Jeremiah 51:45.</note>so that you will not be punished with her because of her perversions.”<verse eID="Rev.18.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.5" osisID="Rev.18.5" n="5"/>“For her perversions are a plague to the heavens and Yehovah will remember her.”<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="498" osisRef="Rev.18.5" osisID="Rev.18.5!note.498">Literally, “You will remember her.”</note><verse eID="Rev.18.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.6" osisID="Rev.18.6" n="6"/>“So, just as she did, they did to her. Then He paid her double, twice as much, according to her works.”<verse eID="Rev.18.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.7" osisID="Rev.18.7" n="7"/>“Then when she boasted and went after her lusts, how they made her suffer. For she devised in her heart, ‘I am queen, and I will not be a widow,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="499" osisRef="Rev.18.7" osisID="Rev.18.7!note.499">Isaiah 47:7-8.</note> nor see sorrow.’”<verse eID="Rev.18.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.8" osisID="Rev.18.8" n="8"/>“For this reason, it will come, their sorrows on one day of death: grief and hunger. And the people will burn by fire because the mighty Master Yehovah will judge her.”<verse eID="Rev.18.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.9" osisID="Rev.18.9" n="9"/>“So they will be ruined over her; the kings of the earth who did fornication with her, when they see the smoke of the fire!”<verse eID="Rev.18.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.10" osisID="Rev.18.10" n="10"/>“And they will stand far away from the terror and say, ‘Woe!’ and ‘Alas!’ The great city Babel! Suddenly, your brokenness comes!”<verse eID="Rev.18.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.11" osisID="Rev.18.11" n="11"/>“The merchants will weep and hiss because no one can buy merchandise any longer:”<verse eID="Rev.18.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.12" osisID="Rev.18.12" n="12"/>“the merchandise of gold, silver, precious stones, gems, silk, all kinds of wood, and instruments of silver, iron, tin, and lead,”<verse eID="Rev.18.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.13" osisID="Rev.18.13" n="13"/>“wine, oil, white bread, grain, oxen, ewes, horses, calves and the souls of man.”<verse eID="Rev.18.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.14" osisID="Rev.18.14" n="14"/>“And the fruit that was satisfaction for your spirit will leave you and all the calculations will not be found again.”<verse eID="Rev.18.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.15" osisID="Rev.18.15" n="15"/>“And the merchants who sold her these things will stand far off and lament over her.”<verse eID="Rev.18.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.16" osisID="Rev.18.16" n="16"/>“Then they will say, ‘Woe!’ and ‘Alas!’ The great city, Babel was clothed in splendor of silk, gold, and precious stones.”<verse eID="Rev.18.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.17" osisID="Rev.18.17" n="17"/>“Because within the hour, all your merchandise will be destroyed: your sailors, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="503" osisRef="Rev.18.17" osisID="Rev.18.17!note.503">This is a quote from Ezekiel 27:27.</note>and seaman, your ship’s carpenter, and barterers of your merchandise. So, all the men of your war stand from far off.”<verse eID="Rev.18.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.18" osisID="Rev.18.18" n="18"/>They will shout when they see the smoke and say, “Who is like the great ruined city?”<verse eID="Rev.18.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.19" osisID="Rev.18.19" n="19"/>Then they will throw ashes on their heads, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="510" osisRef="Rev.18.19" osisID="Rev.18.19!note.510">This is a quote from Job 2:12.</note>shouting, weeping, grieving, and saying, "Woe! Woe! The great city, Babel!” Because from her, all the merchants who had ships were made rich by the merchandise of the sea and now in one hour, everything is destroyed!<verse eID="Rev.18.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.20" osisID="Rev.18.20" n="20"/>“Rejoice, O heavens, His set apart ones and His prophets! For Yehovah avenges your vengeance for her!”<verse eID="Rev.18.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.21" osisID="Rev.18.21" n="21"/>Then a messenger took a great stone, like a millstone, and threw it into the sea and said, “Thus, Babel will sink and not rise up again,”<verse eID="Rev.18.21"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.22" osisID="Rev.18.22" n="22"/>“and will not be heard again, not the sound of joy nor a sound of rejoicing, nor the voice of a bridegroom and the voice of a bride. And no longer will there be any tool of workmanship from the masters of handicrafts, nor will the sound of a breath be heard!”<verse eID="Rev.18.22"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.23" osisID="Rev.18.23" n="23"/>“Nor will a light shine in any den because the merchants were the rulers on the ground; for by witchcraft,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="518" osisRef="Rev.18.23" osisID="Rev.18.23!note.518">Compare the word כֶׁשֶף (kesef) in Numbers 21, meaning “sorceries” in Isaiah 47:12, having the Greek equivalent φαρμακείᾳ (pharmakeia). This seems to imply the great city, Babel, is the center of the creation of toxic pharmaceuticals that relates in some way to witchcraft.</note> they incited all the people.”<verse eID="Rev.18.23"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.18.24" osisID="Rev.18.24" n="24"/>“And in her was found the blood of the holy ones, the prophets, and from all those killed on the ground.”<verse eID="Rev.18.24"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.18"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.19" osisID="Rev.19" n="19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.1" osisID="Rev.19.1" n="1"/>Then afterwards, I heard a voice, from its great companies saying, “HalleluYah! Honor, might, and glory be to Yehovah, our Master.”<verse eID="Rev.19.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.2" osisID="Rev.19.2" n="2"/>“For righteous and true is His judgment who sentenced the great harlot, destroyer of the land with her fornication. For He avenged the blood of His servants from her.”<verse eID="Rev.19.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.3" osisID="Rev.19.3" n="3"/>Then they say once more, “HalleluYah!” And the smoke rises from generation to generation.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="519" osisRef="Rev.19.3" osisID="Rev.19.3!note.519">See Isaiah 34:10.</note><verse eID="Rev.19.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.4" osisID="Rev.19.4" n="4"/>So, the twenty-four elders and the four living creatures fall and pray to Yehovah sitting upon the throne, saying, “Amen! HalleluYah!”<verse eID="Rev.19.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.5" osisID="Rev.19.5" n="5"/>Then a voice comes out from the throne, “HalleluYah! Praise, you servants of Yehovah, praise the name of Yehovah!” For both<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="523" osisRef="Rev.19.5" osisID="Rev.19.5!note.523">An unknown scribe struck through the word שניהם (shnihem) and was included in the translation and signified by strikethrough.</note> all the small and great before Him, fear His wrath!”<verse eID="Rev.19.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.6" osisID="Rev.19.6" n="6"/>And I heard a voice like the voice of His great companies, like the sound of many waters. And it was like great voices saying, “HalleluYah! For Yehovah is mighty!”<verse eID="Rev.19.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.7" osisID="Rev.19.7" n="7"/>“They rejoiced <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="524" osisRef="Rev.19.7" osisID="Rev.19.7!note.524">This word was crossed out by an unknown scribe and a marginal note added. The word was included in the translation.</note>happily and gave Him honor, for the time of the wedding of the Lamb has come and His bride is prepared!”<verse eID="Rev.19.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.8" osisID="Rev.19.8" n="8"/>And He gave her pure, becoming, and beautiful silk to wear.<verse eID="Rev.19.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.9" osisID="Rev.19.9" n="9"/>Then he said to me, "Holy are those called to eat the evening meal with the Lamb.” For he said to me these are the words of Yehovah in truth.”<verse eID="Rev.19.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.10" osisID="Rev.19.10" n="10"/>Then I fell at his feet to pray to him, and he said to me, “See, don’t do this! I am of your friends and of your brothers who have the testimonies of Yeshua. Make your prayer to Yehovah!”<verse eID="Rev.19.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.11" osisID="Rev.19.11" n="11"/>Then I saw the heavens open. And I saw a white horse, and sitting on him was named Faithful and True, for He will judge righteously.<verse eID="Rev.19.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.12" osisID="Rev.19.12" n="12"/>And His eyes were like a flame of fire and on His head were many crowns; and a name was written upon Him that no one knows - only He alone.<verse eID="Rev.19.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.13" osisID="Rev.19.13" n="13"/>And He was dressed in clothing sprinkled with blood and His name was the Word of Yehovah.<verse eID="Rev.19.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.14" osisID="Rev.19.14" n="14"/>And all the armies of the heavens follow Him on white horses, wearing clothing of white silk.<verse eID="Rev.19.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.15" osisID="Rev.19.15" n="15"/>Coming from his mouth is a double-edged sword to strike the people with it. He drives them with a rod of iron<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="528" osisRef="Rev.19.15" osisID="Rev.19.15!note.528">See Psalms 2:9. This phrase is also used in Revelation 2:27a, and 12:5.</note> and in the fierceness of the anger of Yehovah, rebuking honestly for the suffering of the land.<verse eID="Rev.19.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.16" osisID="Rev.19.16" n="16"/>And the name written on His clothes is, “King of the kings, and Lord of the lords.”<verse eID="Rev.19.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.17" osisID="Rev.19.17" n="17"/>Then I saw a messenger standing in the sun and he shouted with a loud voice and said to all the birds under the heavens, “Come! Reap from the evening meal of the great Elohim!”<verse eID="Rev.19.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.18" osisID="Rev.19.18" n="18"/>“So that you eat the flesh of the kings, the overseers, the strong men, the horses and those riding them, even the flesh of the small and the great, the slaves and the free.”<verse eID="Rev.19.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.19" osisID="Rev.19.19" n="19"/>Then I saw the beast and the rulers of the land assemble their armies to make war with Him sitting upon the throne, and with His army.<verse eID="Rev.19.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.20" osisID="Rev.19.20" n="20"/>And the beast was captured with the false prophet, who was performing signs to incite those following who took the sign<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="532" osisRef="Rev.19.20" osisID="Rev.19.20!note.532">This word is “sign,” such as a sign from heaven. This is not the same word for the “sign” of the beast. See interlinear table for more details.</note> of the beast and prayed to him. They were sent to<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="533" osisRef="Rev.19.20" osisID="Rev.19.20!note.533">Literally, “in.” The prefix is ב bet not ל lamed, so it is “in” not “to” and was translated as, “to.”</note> Sheol burning with sulfur.<verse eID="Rev.19.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.19.21" osisID="Rev.19.21" n="21"/>And the others were killed by the sword (which came out of His mouth) of the one who sat on the horse; and all the beasts of the field were satiated with their flesh.<verse eID="Rev.19.21"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.19"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.20" osisID="Rev.20" n="20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.1" osisID="Rev.20.1" n="1"/>Then I saw a messenger descended from the heavens, and he had the key of the deep, and in his hand was a great chain.<verse eID="Rev.20.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.2" osisID="Rev.20.2" n="2"/>And he took the dragon, who is Satan, and bound him for a thousand years.<verse eID="Rev.20.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.3" osisID="Rev.20.3" n="3"/>Then he threw him to the deep, closed <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="538" osisRef="Rev.20.3" osisID="Rev.20.3!note.538">The word וסגור (v’sagor), which is either an adjective or an imperative verb; neither of which make sense in the context. An unknown scribe crossed this word out and wrote in “ וסגר ,” which makes it a verb. The verb was used because it fit the context of the verse. Additionally, it is possible that the scribe erroneously transposed the gimel and the vav which would make the word an active participle meaning “closing.”</note>him in and sealed it from above so that he could not incite the peoples until he had been restrained for a thousand years. Afterwards, he will be free for a short time.<verse eID="Rev.20.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.4" osisID="Rev.20.4" n="4"/>Then I saw thrones, and those sitting upon them. He authorized them to judge those believing and testifying of Yeshua. These are the ones who did not take the sign of the beast upon their foreheads, nor did they pray to him nor to his idol. These are the ones who live and rule with Yeshua for a thousand years.<verse eID="Rev.20.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.5" osisID="Rev.20.5" n="5"/>Indeed, the other dead are not rising until the thousand years of imprisonment are over. But this is the first resurrection.<verse eID="Rev.20.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.6" osisID="Rev.20.6" n="6"/>Holy is he who has a part in the first resurrection, because there is not a snare of another death for them. Only those will be priests<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="542" osisRef="Rev.20.6" osisID="Rev.20.6!note.542">The word כהנים (kohanim) is the word for “priests,” used in the Tanakh. Also see Exodus 19:6, Isa 61:6; 66:21; 1 Peter 2:5,9; Revelation 1:6, 5:10; 20:6.</note> to Yehovah and His messiah and rule for a thousand years.<verse eID="Rev.20.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.7" osisID="Rev.20.7" n="7"/>But after a thousand years, Satan will be freed from his guards,<verse eID="Rev.20.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.8" osisID="Rev.20.8" n="8"/>Then he will go and entice the peoples throughout the four corners of the land, and Gog and Magog, to gather his army for battle: for their number is as the sand of the sea.<verse eID="Rev.20.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.9" osisID="Rev.20.9" n="9"/>And they trampled the wide ground surrounding the soldiers of the holy ones and the beloved city. But fire from the heavens fell and burned them.<verse eID="Rev.20.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.10" osisID="Rev.20.10" n="10"/>And Satan, the inciter, will be thrown into the fire of the Sheol, where the false prophet is. They will be afflicted day and night forever and ever.<verse eID="Rev.20.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.11" osisID="Rev.20.11" n="11"/>Then I saw a great white throne and He who sat upon it. For the heavens and the ground flee before him, and no place is found for him.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="545" osisRef="Rev.20.11" osisID="Rev.20.11!note.545">It is unclear who or what this verse is referring. The word could be translated as either him or it, and the only masculine singular words in the context are the throne and the one sitting on it. It could also be referring to Satan, the Inciter from the previous verse.</note><verse eID="Rev.20.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.12" osisID="Rev.20.12" n="12"/>Then I saw the dead with them, the least and the great, standing before Yehovah, and the scrolls were opened. Then another scroll was opened. And this scroll was the Scroll of Life to judge the dead according to what was written in the scrolls, and according to their works.<verse eID="Rev.20.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.13" osisID="Rev.20.13" n="13"/>Then the sea threw out the dead that were in it. And the Sheol gave the dead to be judged, everyone according to his works.<verse eID="Rev.20.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.14" osisID="Rev.20.14" n="14"/>So, the death and the Gehinnom were thrown <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="548" osisRef="Rev.20.14" osisID="Rev.20.14!note.548">At first glance, it appears that this word נשלך (nushlach) is first person plural future tense, meaning “we will be thrown,” but does not fit the context. However, this word is used in The War of the Jews 2:14:1, and is translated as “they were thrown,” and does fit the context.</note>into the fire: for this (the fire) <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="549" osisRef="Rev.20.14" osisID="Rev.20.14!note.549">This word, זאת (zot) is feminine singular so would be referring or modifying fire ( אש ), which is also feminine singular. It would not modify death ( מות ) because it is masculine singular.</note>is another death.<verse eID="Rev.20.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.20.15" osisID="Rev.20.15" n="15"/>And moreover, if one is not found written in the Scroll of Life <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="550" osisRef="Rev.20.15" osisID="Rev.20.15!note.550">Literally, “scroll of lives,” but usually translated as “book of life.” See also Revelation 3:5 and 20:12.</note>he is thrown into the fire.<verse eID="Rev.20.15"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.20"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.21" osisID="Rev.21" n="21"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.1" osisID="Rev.21.1" n="1"/>Then I saw a new heavens and a new ground, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="551" osisRef="Rev.21.1" osisID="Rev.21.1!note.551">Isaiah 65:17.</note>for the first heavens and the earth perish, and the sea will be no more.<verse eID="Rev.21.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.2" osisID="Rev.21.2" n="2"/>For I, John saw the new, holy city Jerusalem, descend from the heavens to the earth. It was adorned as a woman silent before her husband.<verse eID="Rev.21.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.3" osisID="Rev.21.3" n="3"/>Then I heard a loud voice from the throne that said, “See, the Tabernacle of Yehovah is near mankind! So, He was Elohim to them, and they will be His people.”<verse eID="Rev.21.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.4" osisID="Rev.21.4" n="4"/>And Yehovah Elohim wipes every tear from every face, and death will not reign again, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="554" osisRef="Rev.21.4" osisID="Rev.21.4!note.554">Isaiah 25:8.</note>nor will there be any grief, shouting, or sufferings because the first has passed away.<verse eID="Rev.21.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.5" osisID="Rev.21.5" n="5"/>And the One sitting on the throne said, “See, I made all things new.” And He said to me “Write, for these are the words of truth.”<verse eID="Rev.21.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.6" osisID="Rev.21.6" n="6"/>Then he said to me, “All has passed. I am the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End, and I will freely give living water to the thirsty.”<verse eID="Rev.21.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.7" osisID="Rev.21.7" n="7"/>“He that prevails will inherit everything, and I will be Elohim to him, and he will be My son.”<verse eID="Rev.21.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.8" osisID="Rev.21.8" n="8"/>But, to unbelievers, <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="559" osisRef="Rev.21.8" osisID="Rev.21.8!note.559">Literally, “those without belief.”</note>murderers, harlots, sorcerers, and deceivers, I will give their wages from the fire of the Sheol, burning with fire and sulfur. This is the second death.<verse eID="Rev.21.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.9" osisID="Rev.21.9" n="9"/>Then one of the seven messengers who had the bowls in their hands filled with the seven last plagues came to me and said, “Come and see the woman, the bride of the Lamb!"<verse eID="Rev.21.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.10" osisID="Rev.21.10" n="10"/>And he led me to a mountain that was large and high, and he showed me the great, holy city, Jerusalem. It came down from the heavens to the earth.<verse eID="Rev.21.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.11" osisID="Rev.21.11" n="11"/>The glory of Yehovah was upon her as the light of the precious stone, called Jasper.<verse eID="Rev.21.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.12" osisID="Rev.21.12" n="12"/>And it had a wall and twelve large gates. On the twelve gates were twelve messengers <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="563" osisRef="Rev.21.12" osisID="Rev.21.12!note.563">It appears that this is “kings,” or “rulers” (melekim) and not “messengers” (malakim).</note>and the twelve names of the twelve tribes of Israel<verse eID="Rev.21.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.13" osisID="Rev.21.13" n="13"/>to the east, three gates; to the west, three gates; to the north, three gates; and to the south, three gates.<verse eID="Rev.21.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.14" osisID="Rev.21.14" n="14"/>The wall of the city had twelve foundations and on them were inscribed the names of the twelve sent ones (disciples) of the Lamb.<verse eID="Rev.21.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.15" osisID="Rev.21.15" n="15"/>Then the one that spoke with me had in his hand a measuring rod of gold to measure the city, the wall and the gates.<verse eID="Rev.21.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.16" osisID="Rev.21.16" n="16"/>Then the city was laid out on four sides, its length the same as its width. He measured the city with the measuring-rod as twelve thousand graves<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="567" osisRef="Rev.21.16" osisID="Rev.21.16!note.567">This appears to be a standard of measurement. Graves and sepulchers probably were made all the same length and became a standard measurement of length.</note> of the field, with the city’s length, width and height all equal.<verse eID="Rev.21.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.17" osisID="Rev.21.17" n="17"/>And its walls were one hundred forty and four cubits like the measurement of a man that was to a messenger.<verse eID="Rev.21.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.18" osisID="Rev.21.18" n="18"/>The wall was jasper, and the city was pure gold.<verse eID="Rev.21.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.19" osisID="Rev.21.19" n="19"/>The foundation of the wall and the city was adorned with precious stones. The first foundation was jasper, the second sapphire, the third, chalcedony,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="568" osisRef="Rev.21.19" osisID="Rev.21.19!note.568">The scribe inserted “ בלעז ” (in a foreign tongue”) just above this word. That phrase was not included in the translation since it appears that he is noting that this word is a transliteration of foreign word.</note> the fourth emerald,<verse eID="Rev.21.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.20" osisID="Rev.21.20" n="20"/>the fifth ruby, the sixth onyx, the seventh amethyst, the eighth beryl, the ninth topaz, tenth agate, the eleventh opal, and the twelfth, called in a foreign tongue, amethyst.<verse eID="Rev.21.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.21" osisID="Rev.21.21" n="21"/>The twelve gates were pearls, and each gate was of one pearl. The streets of the city were of unalloyed gold, like a clear plate.<verse eID="Rev.21.21"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.22" osisID="Rev.21.22" n="22"/>But I saw no house of prayer there because Yehovah of Armies was the dwelling place <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="571" osisRef="Rev.21.22" osisID="Rev.21.22!note.571">This word, מקדש (mikdash) is used to refer to the Temple, or dwelling place of Yehovah.</note>for them and the Lamb.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="572" osisRef="Rev.21.22" osisID="Rev.21.22!note.572">See Ezekiel 37:28.</note><verse eID="Rev.21.22"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.23" osisID="Rev.21.23" n="23"/>So, the city had no need of light from the sun or the moon because the glory of Yehovah was their light, and there was the light of the Lamb.<verse eID="Rev.21.23"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.24" osisID="Rev.21.24" n="24"/>And the holy people walk in this light and the kings of the earth bring their wealth to it.<verse eID="Rev.21.24"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.25" osisID="Rev.21.25" n="25"/>The gates were always open day and night and not closed, for there is no night.<verse eID="Rev.21.25"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.26" osisID="Rev.21.26" n="26"/><note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="573" osisRef="Rev.21.26" osisID="Rev.21.26!note.573">There are two different translations because of the word המה (he’ma). If it is literal, then there is an extra pronoun. Thus, since “they” is part of the verb that follows then the sentence begins as, “they come in.” However, if it is used as מה (ma) then it becomes an exclamation.</note>Then they came in with glory and honor of the peoples. And what glory and honor will come in <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="574" osisRef="Rev.21.26" osisID="Rev.21.26!note.574">Literally, “In her.”</note>of the peoples!<verse eID="Rev.21.26"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.21.27" osisID="Rev.21.27" n="27"/>And no strange thing will come near it; only those whose name is written in the Scroll of Life.<verse eID="Rev.21.27"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.21"/>
        <chapter sID="Rev.22" osisID="Rev.22" n="22"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.1" osisID="Rev.22.1" n="1"/>Then a river was shown to me full of living waters - pure, like amethyst, coming from the throne of Yehovah and the Lamb.<verse eID="Rev.22.1"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.2" osisID="Rev.22.2" n="2"/>And in the middle of the open places and from the border of the river: and from this side and the other side - every tree is food; and upon the Tree of Life. And its twelve species of fruit on it is to be for food and its leaves will be medicine.<verse eID="Rev.22.2"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.3" osisID="Rev.22.3" n="3"/>For there will never again be utter destruction. And the throne of Yehovah and the Lamb will dwell in it,<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="576" osisRef="Rev.22.3" osisID="Rev.22.3!note.576">Literally, “In her,” (i.e. the city of Jerusalem).</note> and His servants will minister to Him.<note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="577" osisRef="Rev.22.3" osisID="Rev.22.3!note.577">There are two subjects within this verse: Yehovah and the Lamb. Therefore, according to the grammar, both the verb and the pronouns should be plural to reflect the subjects. However, in the Tanakh, the rule of first mention is that the first verb will reflect the first subject. Yet, this grammatical structure the verb following both subjects and appears to be incorrect.</note><verse eID="Rev.22.3"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.4" osisID="Rev.22.4" n="4"/>And they will see His face, and His name will be written upon their foreheads.<verse eID="Rev.22.4"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.5" osisID="Rev.22.5" n="5"/>And there never again will be the light of the sun in it <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="580" osisRef="Rev.22.5" osisID="Rev.22.5!note.580">Literally, “On her,” (i.e. Jerusalem).</note>by day nor the brightness of the moon. It will not shine for you, for Yehovah is the eternal light, and your Elohim is for your glory.<verse eID="Rev.22.5"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.6" osisID="Rev.22.6" n="6"/>Then He said to me, “These words are truth: Yehovah of Armies sent His messengers to show His servants what will soon be.”<verse eID="Rev.22.6"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.7" osisID="Rev.22.7" n="7"/>See, I come in haste! Holy is he who believes in the words of the prophecy, what is written in this scroll.<verse eID="Rev.22.7"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.8" osisID="Rev.22.8" n="8"/>For I, John, heard and saw all these things. And because of what I heard and saw, I fell to the earth and prayed to the messenger who showed me this.<verse eID="Rev.22.8"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.9" osisID="Rev.22.9" n="9"/>And he said to me, “See! Don’t do this!” I am also from your friends and of your company of brothers, the prophets and of those fulfilling the words of this scroll. Make your prayer to Yehovah!"<verse eID="Rev.22.9"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.10" osisID="Rev.22.10" n="10"/>Then he said to me, “Do not seal the vision of this scroll because the time is near.”<verse eID="Rev.22.10"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.11" osisID="Rev.22.11" n="11"/>“Those who are evil will be for all time. Yes, those who are impure will also be the same way. However, those who are faithful will continue to be so for all time. And whoever is holy is also like this. Truly, like this.”<verse eID="Rev.22.11"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.12" osisID="Rev.22.12" n="12"/>“See, I come in haste to pay every man according to his works!”<verse eID="Rev.22.12"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.13" osisID="Rev.22.13" n="13"/>“I am the First and the Last, the Beginning and the End.”<verse eID="Rev.22.13"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.14" osisID="Rev.22.14" n="14"/>“Holy are they who do His commandments so that they will have a part in the tree of life. Pure, he will walk into the city.”<verse eID="Rev.22.14"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.15" osisID="Rev.22.15" n="15"/>“For outside stands the dog, the sorcerers, the harlots, the murderers, and the deceivers.”<verse eID="Rev.22.15"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.16" osisID="Rev.22.16" n="16"/>“I, Yeshua, I sent messengers to show the congregation. I am the root of the tribes of David.”<verse eID="Rev.22.16"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.17" osisID="Rev.22.17" n="17"/>“And the Spirit and the bride say, ‘Come!’ For whoever hears, says, ‘Come!’ And whoever thirsts, go to the living water and drink without silver and without price.<verse eID="Rev.22.17"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.18" osisID="Rev.22.18" n="18"/>“However, I testify to all the hearers of the prophecy of this scroll: anyone who adds any words <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="584" osisRef="Rev.22.18" osisID="Rev.22.18!note.584">This word, דברים (devarim), can mean words, matters, or things.</note>to it, Yehovah will add to him the plagues written within this scroll.”<verse eID="Rev.22.18"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.19" osisID="Rev.22.19" n="19"/>“And if one removes <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="585" osisRef="Rev.22.19" osisID="Rev.22.19!note.585">This word, יגרע (yigra), does not simply mean remove, it means to diminish or reduce.</note>any words <note type="explanation" placement="foot" n="586" osisRef="Rev.22.19" osisID="Rev.22.19!note.586">See previous verse, this is also the word דברים (devarim).</note>from this scroll, Yehovah will erase his name from the Scroll of Life.”<verse eID="Rev.22.19"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.20" osisID="Rev.22.20" n="20"/>“And thus says this witness to this, ‘Truely, I will come in haste!’ “Amen. Yes, come Master Yeshua!”<verse eID="Rev.22.20"/>
        <verse sID="Rev.22.21" osisID="Rev.22.21" n="21"/>May the grace of our Master Yeshua, the Messiah, be with all of you. Amen!<verse eID="Rev.22.21"/>
        <chapter eID="Rev.22"/>
      </div>
  </osisText>
</osis>
